and a v2 viewer belongs to the owed telemetry plan.\\n- `agent.tzu` and `agent.tools`: delete (D2, flagged for the owner). The LLM call is blocking `take!!` over a v1 HTTP transport v2 cannot offer, and the tool protocol exposes integer cursor positions to the model. The honest v2 shape is the Yin-native agent the harness doc already describes.\\n- `yin.module` family: delete (D3, flagged for the owner since it retires a capability nothing can exercise).\\n\\n**Units carrying real design risk:** U3 and U4.\\n- U3 rewrites `dao.postgraphics.terminal` from wake-on-append with waiters into a step-driven binding, with each host widget owning a ticker (the same shape as the v2 Flutter REPL widget). Code is small, but verification is manual across five Flutter entries and four browser routes.\\n- U4 keeps `dao.gui.event`'s driver as is and changes about sixty lines, but its 2,362-line spec requires a reject-mode input stream that v2 refuses to build. The backpressure section is rewritten to \\\"refuses and parks, or evicts and reports gap\\\", with the park path tested over scripted handles.\\n- U7, the rename wave, is bigger than R4 estimated (163 files) and carries one protocol decision: the v2 apply and RPC wire keywords and the ws subprotocol string contain the namespace. Flagged for the owner, recommendation is a clean break.\\n\\n**Can it start now:** yes. U1 (fourteen orphan files and three shadow builds) can land today with no design content. U3 and U4 are independent lanes that touch different regions of the two artifact demo files. D2 and D3 gate only U2.\\n\\n**One-line sequencing recommendation:** land U1 today, answer D2/D3 and land U2 this week, run U3 and U4 in parallel with the artifact demo as their shared integration test, then U5, U6, U7 in one sitting so two stream namespaces never coexist longer than necessary.\",\"ttft_ms\":3390,\"type\":\"result\",\"duration_ms\":703190,\"uuid\":\"3986cc05-9d82-45b6-8d14-ee31dd75a903\",\"ttft_stream_ms\":2252,\"time_to_request_ms\":42,\"first_content_frame_ms\":2252,\"queued_turn_count\":0,\"result_index\":0}\ncollab/dao-stream/1790237091-reviewer-repl-vm-stream-r2.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:15:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n -A8 -B5 'image.hash|image-hash|:hash.*loaded|same-image|H is|H:' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | head -n 145\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"61-free name resolves through primitives or modules, and none is shadowed by the\\n62-receiver's free environment or store. An image with no `:load-free` operands\\n63-passes trivially. Otherwise B6 refuses it with `:unresolved-free` or\\n64-`:shadowed-free`, rather than executing it.\\n65-\\n66:H is alpha-invariant for binder renames because binder names are outside the\\n67-hash. Equal H therefore implies agreement on exact scalar spelling and free\\n68-name operands as well as the same executable image. Alpha-equivalent source\\n69-programs share H only when their front-end tail flags agree; this is sound but\\n70-intentionally incomplete.\\n71-\\n72-The following are not promised to be identical: binder spelling in the\\n73-dormant projection, instruction program counters, temporary ids, allocation\\n74-identity, stack-trace text, source-map formatting, or interchangeability of\\n--\\n170-domain are refused with a qualified `unsupported-value` diagnostic, not\\n171-silently canonicalized. This is deliberately distinct\\n172-from `yin.vm.debruijn/encode-value`.\\n173-\\n174-Binder names and provenance are carried in a diagnostic side table indexed by\\n175:instruction slot and are outside the image hash. Exact scalar bytes are in\\n176:the `:const` operands and are included in the image hash; free names remain\\n177-hashed `:load-free` operands.\\n178-\\n179-Host classification is explicit. On CLJS, a JavaScript number is encoded as a\\n180-long when it is a safe integer and as a double otherwise. Integral-valued\\n181-doubles are outside the CLJS common scalar domain, because JavaScript cannot\\n182-distinguish them from longs; a CLJS receiver refuses such an image with\\n183-`unsupported-value`. CLJS cannot distinguish source spellings that already\\n184-read as the same number. Characters are a distinct class only where the\\n--\\n199-operands. A producer can scan those tags to identify common-domain-safe images.\\n200-A receiving host that lacks a class refuses the image with a qualified\\n201-`unsupported-value` outcome before execution; this is a host-boundary outcome,\\n202-not a stream gap.\\n203-\\n204:The code-image identity H is computed by one B1 function, `image-hash`, over\\n205-the descriptor hash, including its lowering-contract version, and the\\n206-canonical positional instruction vector: pc-indexed tuples, refs resolved to\\n207-pcs, no header, and exact scalar bytes. Provenance is\\n208:a diagnostic side table indexed by pc and is outside the hash. H is the only\\n209-executable and sharing identity. No projection fingerprint or second identity\\n210-is attached to the image, request, response, verification, or cache. Any later\\n211-cache is keyed by H.\\n212-No cache is specified by B0 through B5; any later cache is an explicit value\\n213-keyed by H. A receiver hashes the received canonical wire bytes before\\n214-decoding; those bytes are H's preimage. `jing/segment-key` is only the storage\\n215-address for the same bytes and is not H; `yin.vm.content` stores and fetches\\n216:bytes, while verification recomputes `image-hash`. H is computed using explicit\\n217-SHA-256 and is pinned by the VM format contract version; it is completely\\n218-independent of DaoJing's `default-hash-algorithm` and does not change when\\n219-DaoJing defaults to BLAKE3.\\n220-\\n221-## 3. Lowering and scope\\n222-\\n223-Lowering is two stages, both pure stream-to-stream functions:\\n224-\\n--\\n470-   `:yin/blocked` (both done by the engine). B3's `(peek stack)` is\\n471-   equivalent for pure programs and stays as the halting write.\\n472-3. Register payload. `{:segment :pc :frames :stack :continuation :format\\n473-   :hash}`. `:segment` names the image as the record holds it, in the same\\n474-   role as the semantic VM's segment id; while one image is loaded it is\\n475:   that image. `:format` is `:yin.debruijn.code` and `:hash` is the loaded\\n476:   image's H: every parked record and reified continuation carries the\\n477-   model and image identity that produced it, so a continuation is never\\n478-   interpreted by a VM or against an image other than its own. These keys\\n479-   do not collide with the engine-owned set and the engine never reads\\n480-   them. Free environment, store, primitives, and modules are not\\n481-   registers and stay on the state map.\\n482-4. Restore. One function, `stack-restore [base entry val]`, which first\\n483-   refuses with a qualified `:continuation-format` outcome unless the\\n484-   entry's `:format` is `:yin.debruijn.code` and its `:hash` equals the\\n485-   loaded image's H, then writes the registers from the entry and conjes\\n486-   `val` onto the restored `:stack` (the B3 obligation that every\\n487-   value-producing opcode lands on the stack, since there is no\\n488:   accumulator). The refusal is the same-model, same-image rule the\\n489-   register design states in its section 5.1; cross-model transport is\\n490-   not a lift here and is deliberately unsupported. The FFI two-step\\n491-   (engine design section 4) lives here, on the entry keys `:request-sent`\\n492-   and `:call-id`, using `ffi/call-result` and `ffi/response-wait-entry`.\\n493-   The walker's frame-typed placement is not used: this machine's\\n494-   continuation is a vector of return frames.\\n495-5. Builders. Per blocking instruction, a `:stream/put` and `:stream/next`\\n496-   builder closing over the post-instruction registers (`pc` advanced,\\n--\\n548-    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc\\n549-    Existing edits: none\\n550-    Must not change: yin.vm.code, :yin.code/*, merged projection namespace\\n551-\\n552-Define and export the descriptor, derived opcode table, exact executable\\n553:scalar encoder, code-image hash, and validator. Completion includes malformed\\n554-rows,\\n555-out-of-range bound operands, exact spelling preservation, distinct hashes\\n556-for composed and decomposed e-acute, a ratio fixture that is either\\n557-encoded or refused with `:unsupported-value` (JVM encodes, CLJS and\\n558-ClojureDart both refuse), distinct hashes for `1`/`1.0` on JVM and Dart,\\n559-distinct hashes for ratios and chars on the JVM, and identical bytes\\n560-across hosts for the common scalar domain. Golden image bytes and H\\n561-values for a frozen corpus are checked on all three host lanes. A\\n--\\n680-    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\\n681-    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\\n682-    Existing edits: none\\n683-    Depends on: dao.jing (segment-key, materialize!, get), dao.jing.dht\\n684-    (create-content-dht, IDhtNet), dao.jing.remote (the DaoStream\\n685:    transport and its server handlers), B1 (image-hash, image-defect,\\n686-    descriptor), B2 (lift)\\n687:    Must not change: merged projection namespace, image-hash, dao.stream,\\n688-    dao.jing, dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote, named VM semantics\\n689-\\n690-B6 is specified in `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, the\\n691-standalone linker design for Phase B6, used by both the stack and register\\n692-VM for linking code over `dao.stream`. That document specifies the format-\\n693-parameterized fetch function over `dao.stream` / `dao.jing`, the address and\\n694-identity distinction, the six-step fetch protocol, the qualified refusal\\n695-vocabulary, the H and R indexes, the transitional content-hash risk, and the\\n--\\n712-\\n713-The matrix includes exact scalar spelling, names, tail flags,\\n714-provenance, scope, duplicate parameters, nil fill, extras dropped, control\\n715-flow, streams, effects, continuations, macros, malformed images, and all\\n716-existing parity/content/completion execution fixtures. It also includes\\n717:alpha-equivalent binder renames with equal image hashes, exact-spelling and\\n718-tail-flag differences with distinct hashes, free-name changes, stream transfer,\\n719-and cross-host code-image bytes.\\n720-\\n721-Non-goals are equality saturation, a new JIT, global distribution beyond B6\\n722-and B7, and any change to the merged dormant projection or its fingerprint.\\n723-\\n724-### 7.1 Prior art: Unison's runtime\\n725-\\n--\\n782-decision.\\n783-\\n784-`dao.jing` stores and fetches the image as a value at its own content\\n785-address, over `dao.stream` through `dao.jing.remote`. B verifies the\\n786-received value twice, against its Jing address with `segment-key` and\\n787:against H with `image-hash`, and never treats `jing/segment-key` as H. The\\n788-integrity rule is the same as hashing wire bytes before trusting them; the\\n789-mechanics are Jing's, which hash the decoded value, and the B6 box states\\n790-them. `load-vector` and `:code-aliases` remain named-VM machinery. B6 does\\n791-not require persistent publication or discovery: the composition supplies\\n792-the Jing handle and the H index.\\n793-\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-stream/1791380000000-architect-stream-s2d-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:3384:+S2d is **not approved for final architectural sign-off in its current form**. Resolve B1 and B2 with explicit host-supported teardown semantics, accurate host-gate documentation, and tests that exercise host restrictions. Tighten the same-tick adoption assertion during reconciliation. The bulk of the portable bounds work is acceptable; neither successful local outcome tests nor the current host matrix demonstrates the promised cross-host teardown.\ncollab/dao-stream/1791380000000-architect-stream-s2d-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:3458:+S2d is **not approved for final architectural sign-off in its current form**. Resolve B1 and B2 with explicit host-supported teardown semantics, accurate host-gate documentation, and tests that exercise host restrictions. Tighten the same-tick adoption assertion during reconciliation. The bulk of the portable bounds work is acceptable; neither successful local outcome tests nor the current host matrix demonstrates the promised cross-host teardown.\ncollab/dao-stream/1791380000000-architect-stream-s2d-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:3538:+S2d is **not approved for final architectural sign-off in its current form**. Resolve B1 and B2 with explicit host-supported teardown semantics, accurate host-gate documentation, and tests that exercise host restrictions. Tighten the same-tick adoption assertion during reconciliation. The bulk of the portable bounds work is acceptable; neither successful local outcome tests nor the current host matrix demonstrates the promised cross-host teardown.\ncollab/dao-stream/1792542000000-architect-codec-c4.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:29:| Lane counts vs C3 | JVM 503 = C3 503; Node 506 = C3 506 (from the C3 fixes report). Dart 435 has no C3 baseline in the collab reports I could find; the brief reports it green for every codec/cbor/transit/ws namespace with the one known Linux datagram gap |\ncollab/dao-stream/1792542000000-architect-codec-c4.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:98:the C3 counts (503, 506, Dart baseline to be named).\ncollab/dao-stream/1789007994328-stream-jing-remote-phase1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:17:| kondo `remote.cljc` | **0 errors, 0 warnings** (baseline also 0/0) | — |\ncollab/dao-stream/1789007994328-stream-jing-remote-phase1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:23:**One deviation, reported not smuggled:** only 2 of the plan's 6 new requires were added. `dao.stream.rpc.ws` under its natural alias is mechanically impossible while v1's require stands — `IllegalStateException: Alias rpc-ws already exists` (demonstrated) — and `dao.stream`/`dao.stream.transit` have zero Phase 1 call sites, so they'd fail the kondo lane whose baseline is clean. All three are §10 end-state requires that land with Phase 2 when v1 goes. Also worth knowing: with a call in flight, a bare `/detached` yields `:done` + a `:reason` completion (N9's loss) rather than `:terminal` — that's the verbatim step's behaviour, and both shapes are now pinned.\ncollab/dao-stream/1792482930000-architect-codec-f0-doc-amendment.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:5:Task: Record the F0 baseline-exceptions amendment in docs/design/dao.stream.codec.md §7.3\ncollab/dao-stream/1792482930000-architect-codec-f0-doc-amendment.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:11:everywhere and CBOR re-encodes byte-exact) and the weak claim (baseline\ncollab/dao-stream/1792482930000-architect-codec-f0-doc-amendment.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:22:2. Baseline exceptions (`exceptions.edn`): pre-change cross-host divergence and\ncollab/dao-stream/1791492159522-stream-engineer-stream-s5.prompt.md:16:- S4 baseline & sign-off: `archive/stream-crossmachine-s4/1791403500000-architect-stream-s4-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`, `archive/stream-crossmachine-s4/1791405000000-architect-stream-s4-signoff.codex.findings.md`\ncollab/dao-stream/1789015301045-stream-jing-remote-phase2-r3.glm-5.3.findings.md:83:| command | outcome | r2 baseline |\ncollab/dao-stream/1789281332481-review-macro-stream-process-r8.glm-5.3.stdout.log:27:**[P3] Two implementation cautions the doc should pin, because the determinism claim is absolute (\"same inputs, same output, on every host\") and both have a silent wrong-implementation available.** (a) Harvest ordinals and admission's *first reported* violation must iterate the **datom vector**, not the `group-by` index map — index-map iteration order is host-dependent (and on cljs is not insertion order), which would make catalogue ordinals and the `:eid` in a `:malformed-input` report vary across hosts. The check *set* is order-independent; the *ordinal assignment* and *error selection* are not. (b) T