ariants of §7.4.3.\\\\n1514:   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\\\\n1579:## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\\\\n1596:- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\\\\n1649:### 7.11.1 Blocker-closure acceptance matrix\\\\n1703:  effectful calls, and halt; also exercise M4's `:link-request`,\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_13\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'defn .*append-link-request|defn .*poll|:request|:response|:cursor' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_13\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'defn .*append-link-request|defn .*poll|:request|:response|:cursor' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"270:  {:effect :stream/next, :cursor c})\\\\n397:(defn append-link-request\\\\n475:                         :request link-request-resource\\\\n476:                         :response link-response-resource\\\\n477:                         :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor minted))]\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_14\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The three reported changes are present, including frame-wide cell remapping. I’m checking whether lowered entries also satisfy the engine paths that consume them; the linker appears to have a resource-key dependency that may matter here.\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_15\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '385,420p;450,485p' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc | nl -ba -v385\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_15\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '385,420p;450,485p' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc | nl -ba -v385\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"   385\\\\t   :blocked? true})\\\\n   386\\\\t\\\\n   387\\\\t\\\\n   388\\\\t(defn- mint-link-id\\\\n   389\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The next link id `[origin counter]` of this task (section 7.2, step 3):\\\\n   390\\\\t   the task's origin tag beside its own engine counter.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   391\\\\t  [state]\\\\n   392\\\\t  (let [counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)]\\\\n   393\\\\t    [[(or (:origin state) :t0) counter]\\\\n   394\\\\t     (assoc state :id-counter (inc counter))]))\\\\n   395\\\\t\\\\n   396\\\\t\\\\n   397\\\\t(defn append-link-request\\\\n   398\\\\t  \\\\\\\"Append `entry`'s retained envelope on the link request stream. `ok`\\\\n   399\\\\t   moves the entry to the `:link-response` state; `full` keeps it in\\\\n   400\\\\t   `:link-request`, envelope verbatim, for the next poll; the terminal\\\\n   401\\\\t   outcomes throw, naming the outcome and the link id.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   402\\\\t  [resources entry]\\\\n   403\\\\t  (let [writer (get resources link-request-resource)\\\\n   404\\\\t        o (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! writer (:envelope entry)))]\\\\n   405\\\\t    (case o\\\\n   406\\\\t      :dao.stream/ok (-> entry\\\\n   407\\\\t                         (dissoc :envelope)\\\\n   408\\\\t                         (assoc :reason :link-response))\\\\n   409\\\\t      :dao.stream/full entry\\\\n   410\\\\t      (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"Link request could not be appended\\\\\\\"\\\\n   411\\\\t                      {:outcome o, :link-id (:link-id entry)})))))\\\\n   412\\\\t\\\\n   413\\\\t\\\\n   414\\\\t(defn require-handler\\\\n   415\\\\t  \\\\\\\"Resolve `:module/require` (yin.vm.linker.md sections 7.2 and 8.3).\\\\n   416\\\\t\\\\n   417\\\\t   A module already in the registry value -- a host module, or one linked\\\\n   418\\\\t   earlier and lowered into this task -- answers now. A module installing\\\\n   419\\\\t   in this scheduler (section 7.3) is neither linked nor absent: the\\\\n   420\\\\t   requiring continuation joins that install's waiters as an `:install`\\\\n   421\\\\t\\\\n   422\\\\t      (installing? state module-name)\\\\n   423\\\\t      (block-on state (assoc (registers) :reason :install :name module-name))\\\\n   424\\\\t\\\\n   425\\\\t      (not (and response (get resources link-request-resource)))\\\\n   426\\\\t      (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"Module is not in this VM's registry\\\\\\\"\\\\n   427\\\\t                      {:module module-name,\\\\n   428\\\\t                       :available (vec (list-modules (:modules state)))}))\\\\n   429\\\\t\\\\n   430\\\\t      :else\\\\n   431\\\\t      (let [minted (stream/cursor response stream/anchor-newest)\\\\n   432\\\\t            _ (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome minted))\\\\n   433\\\\t                (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"Link response cursor mint failed\\\\\\\"\\\\n   434\\\\t                                {:outcome (:dao.stream/outcome minted),\\\\n   435\\\\t                                 :module module-name})))\\\\n   436\\\\t            [link-id state] (mint-link-id state)\\\\n   437\\\\t            {:keys [format contract]} (link-format state)\\\\n   438\\\\t            entry (assoc (registers)\\\\n   439\\\\t                         :reason :link-request\\\\n   440\\\\t                         :name module-name\\\\n   441\\\\t                         :link-id link-id\\\\n   442\\\\t                         :envelope {:yin.link/id link-id,\\\\n   443\\\\t                                    :yin.link/name module-name,\\\\n   444\\\\t                                    :yin.link/format format,\\\\n   445\\\\t                                    :yin.link/contract contract}\\\\n   446\\\\t                         :request link-request-resource\\\\n   447\\\\t                         :response link-response-resource\\\\n   448\\\\t                         :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor minted))]\\\\n   449\\\\t        (block-on state (append-link-request resources entry))))))\\\\n   450\\\\t\\\\n   451\\\\t\\\\n   452\\\\t(defn default-registry\\\\n   453\\\\t  \\\\\\\"A registry with the built-in effect handlers and no modules.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   454\\\\t  []\\\\n   455\\\\t  (-> (empty-registry)\\\\n   456\\\\t      (register-effect-handler :module/require require-handler)))\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_16\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'link-request-resource|link-response-resource|defn.*settle|defn.*start-install' src/cljc/yin/vm/{module,engine}.cljc\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_17\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '545,695p;885,945p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_16\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'link-request-resource|link-response-resource|defn.*settle|defn.*start-install' src/cljc/yin/vm/{module,engine}.cljc\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1148:(defn- start-install\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1162:(defn- settle\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1215:  (let [reader (get (:resources state) module/link-response-resource)]\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:360:(def link-request-resource\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:366:(def link-response-resource\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:403:  (let [writer (get resources link-request-resource)\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:440:        response (get resources link-response-resource)]\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:454:      (not (and response (get resources link-request-resource)))\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:475:                         :request link-request-resource\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:476:                         :response link-response-resource\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_17\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '545,695p;885,945p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"     1\\\\t### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\\\\n     2\\\\t\\\\n     3\\\\tA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\\\\n     4\\\\tthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\\\\n     5\\\\tthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\\\\n     6\\\\t*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot. The first draft carried sketches;\\\\n     7\\\\tthe set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\\\\n     8\\\\teverything a *foreign* resumer needs — including the state the first draft\\\\n     9\\\\tleft implicit on the emitter's heap:\\\\n    10\\\\t\\\\n    11\\\\t```clojure\\\\n    12\\\\t;; blocked read (:stream-next)\\\\n    13\\\\t{:yin.k/reason   :next\\\\n    14\\\\t :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\\\\n    15\\\\t\\\\n    16\\\\t;; blocked write (:stream-put, or an effectful call's :stream/put)\\\\n    17\\\\t{:yin.k/reason   :put\\\\n    18\\\\t :yin.k/stream   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; the target\\\\n    19\\\\t :yin.k/value    <encoded>}            ; the retained value — a retry appends THIS, and\\\\n    20\\\\t                                       ; without it the wait is not re-establishable\\\\n    21\\\\t\\\\n    22\\\\t;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\\\\n    23\\\\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\\\\n    24\\\\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\\\\n    25\\\\t :yin.k/op         :op/add\\\\n    26\\\\t :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\\\\n    27\\\\t :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\\\\n    28\\\\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\\\\n    29\\\\t\\\\n    30\\\\t;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\\\\n    31\\\\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\\\\n    32\\\\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\\\\n    33\\\\t :yin.k/request-op :op/add\\\\n    34\\\\t :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\\\\n    35\\\\t :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\\\\n    36\\\\t :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\\\\n    37\\\\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\\\\n    38\\\\t```\\\\n    39\\\\t\\\\n    40\\\\tThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\\\\n    41\\\\t\\\\n    42\\\\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\\\\n    43\\\\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\\\\n    44\\\\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\\\\n    45\\\\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\\\\n    46\\\\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\\\\n    47\\\\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\\\\n    48\\\\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\\\\n    49\\\\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\\\\n    50\\\\t  and Gaps*). At migration the composition serves every carried stream\\\\n    51\\\\t  cell through `dao.stream.remote.md`: the exporter enters the cell's\\\\n    52\\\\t  handle in its table and the cell's stream descriptor becomes a remote\\\\n    53\\\\t  descriptor. This is an access path to the original logical stream, not\\\\n    54\\\\t  a copied stream with new positions. `dao.stream` itself assumes no\\\\n    55\\\\t  network. The mirror serves the source's declared reader and writer\\\\n    56\\\\t  surface, preserving its outcome maps and cursor namespace rather than\\\\n    57\\\\t  assigning new positions. The cell carries its kept cursor. The receiver\\\\n    58\\\\t  attaches a reflection through that descriptor and resumes at the kept\\\\n    59\\\\t  cursor. `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` in `:yin.k/requires` names\\\\n    60\\\\t  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, the one profile: a cursor is plain data in the\\\\n    61\\\\t  channel's portable domain and the source honors it after\\\\n    62\\\\t  serialization, including cursor identity, position, and `gap` outcomes.\\\\n    63\\\\t  If the source cannot be entered in a table, preserve the cursor, or\\\\n    64\\\\t  keep a channel reachable, lift refuses as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before\\\\n    65\\\\t  minting the value. A reflection marked gone on the receiver is also\\\\n    66\\\\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, naming the stream identity. The exporter keeps\\\\n    67\\\\t  the entry served under a lease the resumer holds\\\\n    68\\\\t  (`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6). Re-homing a stream is out of\\\\n    69\\\\t  scope: serving from the exporter pins the exporter.\\\\n    70\\\\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\\\\n    71\\\\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\\\\n    72\\\\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\\\\n    73\\\\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\\\\n    74\\\\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\\\\n    75\\\\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\\\\n    76\\\\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\\\\n    77\\\\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\\\\n    78\\\\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\\\\n    79\\\\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\\\\n    80\\\\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\\\\n    81\\\\t- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\\\\n    82\\\\t  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\\\\n    83\\\\t  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\\\\n    84\\\\t  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\\\\n    85\\\\t  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\\\\n    86\\\\t  value), where through a reflection `ok` is acceptance on the outbound\\\\n    87\\\\t  path and the wait resumes only when the source's `ok` is observed on the\\\\n    88\\\\t  link's event writer, while an append whose effect is unknown leaves the\\\\n    89\\\\t  wait undischarged for the program or its composition to decide\\\\n    90\\\\t  (`dao.stream.remote.md`, sections 2.4 and 2.5); a `:next` wait polls\\\\n    91\\\\t  its cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\\\\n    92\\\\t  retained value is recomputed — what parks is what resumes.\\\\n    93\\\\t\\\\n    94\\\\t**Linker pending variants (M4).** Specified by `yin.vm.linker.md` and\\\\n    95\\\\timplemented in M4. The require flow adds three pending variants, one per\\\\n    96\\\\tsafepoint of the same name (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.2, 7.3). A wait entry's\\\\n    97\\\\t`:cursor` (the kept cursor) is UCF's `:yin.k/cell`: the cell id stands for\\\\n    98\\\\tthe cursor and its kept position rides in the cells table of section\\\\n    99\\\\t7.5.3:\\\\n   100\\\\t\\\\n   101\\\\t```clojure\\\\n   102\\\\t;; module link, request in hand (:module/require miss)\\\\n   103\\\\t{:yin.k/reason    :link-request\\\\n   104\\\\t :yin.k/link-id   [:t0 7]                 ; [origin counter], below\\\\n   105\\\\t :yin.k/envelope  {:yin.link/id [:t0 7] :yin.link/name 'foo\\\\n   106\\\\t                   :yin.link/format :yin.debruijn.code\\\\n   107\\\\t                   :yin.link/contract \\\\\\\"b2\\\\\\\"}   ; rebuilt verbatim\\\\n   108\\\\t :yin.k/request   {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\\\\n   109\\\\t :yin.k/response  {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\\\\n   110\\\\t :yin.k/cell      :yin.k/c-31}            ; response cell, minted newest\\\\n   111\\\\t                                          ; before the request is appended\\\\n   112\\\\t\\\\n   113\\\\t;; module link, awaiting the correlated response (request appended ok)\\\\n   114\\\\t{:yin.k/reason    :link-response\\\\n   115\\\\t :yin.k/link-id   [:t0 7]\\\\n   116\\\\t :yin.k/response  {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\\\\n   117\\\\t :yin.k/cell      :yin.k/c-31}            ; the kept cell, at its kept\\\\n   118\\\\t                                          ; position\\\\n   119\\\\t\\\\n   120\\\\t;; module install (a matching ok discharged; the child runs)\\\\n   121\\\\t{:yin.k/reason    :install\\\\n   122\\\\t :yin.k/name      'foo}\\\\n   123\\\\t```\\\\n   124\\\\t\\\\n   125\\\\tFour rules fix the exchange. The link id is the pair `[origin\\\\n   126\\\\tcounter]`, scoped to the link pair, not to a VM: several tasks share\\\\n   127\\\\tone response stream, `origin` is a task origin tag the scheduler mints\\\\n   128\\\\tfrom its own counter and never reuses, and `counter` is the task's\\\\n   129\\\\tengine gensym, so no two requests on one pair share an id. The response\\\\n   130\\\\tcursor is minted `:dao.stream/newest` before the request is appended --\\\\n   131\\\\tthe `dao.stream.md` *Cursors* rule for observing events caused by an\\\\n   132\\\\toperation -- so a response that lands before the cursor exists is not\\\\n   133\\\\tskipped. `:link-request` carries everything needed to rebuild the\\\\n   134\\\\tenvelope, `:link-response` carries the response descriptor and the kept\\\\n   135\\\\tcell; a `full` append keeps the request state, retains the envelope\\\\n   136\\\\tverbatim, and retries on the next poll, the `:ffi-request` discipline.\\\\n   137\\\\tAnd the poll restores only on exact correlation: the entry advances\\\\n   138\\\\tpast every response whose `:yin.link/id` is not its own, keeping its\\\\n   139\\\\tcursor at the first unconsumed position -- the per-cell round-order\\\\n   140\\\\tdiscipline section 7.5.3 already gives shared cursors -- and skips\\\\n   141\\\\tduplicate, late, and unknown responses, each skip a telemetry\\\\n   142\\\\tdiagnostic that consumes nothing but the cursor position. A matching\\\\n   143\\\\trefusal, or a failed discharge, resumes the program with the refusal\\\\n   144\\\\traised as the effect's error, the way an `:ffi` `error` raises; a\\\\n   145\\\\tcomposition that abandons a link raises the reason the same way and\\\\n   146\\\\tretires the id. A matching `ok` makes the entry `:install`, and no task\\\\n   147\\\\tis restored until the install reaches `linked` or `refused`: `linked`\\\\n   148\\\\trestores every waiter with the required module's symbol as its value,\\\\n   149\\\\t`refused` with the refusal as the effect's error. On `gap` the honest\\\\n   150\\\\toutcome is a `gap`, and the composition's policy decides.\\\\n   151\\\\t\\\\n   152\\\\t### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\\\\n   153\\\\t\\\\n   154\\\\t**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\\\\n   155\\\\tcaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\\\\n   156\\\\talso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\\\\n   157\\\\tduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\\\\n   158\\\\t\\\\n   159\\\\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\\\\n   160\\\\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\\\\n   161\\\\t  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\\\\n   162\\\\t  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\\\\n   163\\\\t  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\\\\n   164\\\\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\\\\n   165\\\\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\\\\n   166\\\\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\\\\n   167\\\\t  for correctness of sharing.\\\\n   168\\\\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\\\\n   169\\\\t  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\\\\n   170\\\\t  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\\\\n   171\\\\t  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\\\\n   172\\\\t  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\\\\n   173\\\\t  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\\\\n   174\\\\t  draft's two competing ones.\\\\n   175\\\\t\\\\n   176\\\\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\\\\n   177\\\\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\\\\n   178\\\\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\\\\n   179\\\\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\\\\n   180\\\\ta single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\\\\n   181\\\\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\\\\n   182\\\\t\\\\n   183\\\\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\\\\n   184\\\\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\\\\n   185\\\\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\\\\n   186\\\\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\\\\n   187\\\\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\\\\n   188\\\\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\\\\n   189\\\\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\\\\n   190\\\\texactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\\\\n   191\\\\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\\\\n   192\\\\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\\\\n   193\\\\t\\\\n   194\\\\t```clojure\\\\n   195\\\\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\\\\n   196\\\\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\\\\n   197\\\\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\\\\n   198\\\\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\\\\n   199\\\\t```\\\\n   200\\\\t\\\\n   201\\\\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\\\\n   202\\\\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\\\\n   203\\\\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\\\\n   204\\\\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\\\\n   205\\\\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\\\\n   206\\\\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\\\\n   207\\\\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\\\\n   208\\\\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\\\\n   209\\\\t\\\\n   210\\\\t**Cells lower into the private resource table (linker M4).** Specified by\\\\n   211\\\\t`yin.vm.linker.md` and implemented in M4. The lowering rule above -- each\\\\n   212\\\\tcell becomes a fresh store cursor entry -- is amended by the resource\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_18\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '1,395p' test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc | nl -ba\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_18\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '1,395p' test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc | nl -ba\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"     1\\\\t(ns yin.vm.ucf.remote-test\\\\n     2\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The slice 8 acceptance row\\\\n     3\\\\t   (`docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md` slice 8;\\\\n     4\\\\t   `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` S7.4.3, S7.5.3, S7.11.1\\\\n     5\\\\t   'Reflection acceptance'): a REAL machine parked through the engine\\\\n     6\\\\t   on a `:stream-next` wait migrates with a kept cursor and resumes\\\\n     7\\\\t   equivalently in a fresh engine state through a reflection; forced\\\\n     8\\\\t   eviction yields `gap` with the source's own recovery cursor; a\\\\n     9\\\\t   kept cursor that cannot survive the channel codec refuses the lift\\\\n    10\\\\t   before any cell is published; a source `serve!` refuses is\\\\n    11\\\\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; an `attach!` that answers gone is\\\\n    12\\\\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream at lower. Cursor cells are\\\\n    13\\\\t   minted and remapped across a whole frame set: two waiters on one\\\\n    14\\\\t   engine cell lift to one UCF cell and lower back shared, and\\\\n    15\\\\t   independent cells at one position stay independent (S7.5.3). The\\\\n    16\\\\t   remaining S7.4.3 variants -- `:put`, `:ffi`, `:ffi-request`,\\\\n    17\\\\t   `:link-request`, `:link-response`, `:install` -- round-trip\\\\n    18\\\\t   through stub `serve!`/`attach!` callbacks over the engine's own\\\\n    19\\\\t   wait-set shapes, since their proof is the shape of the bridge, not\\\\n    20\\\\t   the transport.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    21\\\\t  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\\\\n    22\\\\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\\\\n    23\\\\t            [dao.stream.apply :as apply2]\\\\n    24\\\\t            [dao.stream.remote :as remote]\\\\n    25\\\\t            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\\\\n    26\\\\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\\\\n    27\\\\t            [yin.vm.linearize :as linearize]\\\\n    28\\\\t            [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\\\\n    29\\\\t            [yin.vm.ucf.remote :as ucf.remote]))\\\\n    30\\\\t\\\\n    31\\\\t\\\\n    32\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n    33\\\\t;; The toy: two in-process ring buffers as the channel (as\\\\n    34\\\\t;; dao.stream.remote-test), with a served-table `serve!` on top\\\\n    35\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n    36\\\\t\\\\n    37\\\\t(defn- ring\\\\n    38\\\\t  [capacity]\\\\n    39\\\\t  (:dao.stream/handle\\\\n    40\\\\t    (ringbuffer/create! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ringbuffer\\\\n    41\\\\t                         :dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity capacity})))\\\\n    42\\\\t\\\\n    43\\\\t\\\\n    44\\\\t(defn- toy\\\\n    45\\\\t  []\\\\n    46\\\\t  (let [ab (ring 64)\\\\n    47\\\\t        ba (ring 64)\\\\n    48\\\\t        cd {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream.test/channel\\\\n    49\\\\t            :dao.stream/identity \\\\\\\"ucf-remote-toy\\\\\\\"}]\\\\n    50\\\\t    {:channel cd, :ab ab, :ba ba,\\\\n    51\\\\t     :a-end {:reader ba, :writer ab},\\\\n    52\\\\t     :b-end {:reader ab, :writer ba}}))\\\\n    53\\\\t\\\\n    54\\\\t\\\\n    55\\\\t(defn- served-peer\\\\n    56\\\\t  [t]\\\\n    57\\\\t  {:table (atom {})\\\\n    58\\\\t   :b-reader (:reader (:b-end t))\\\\n    59\\\\t   :b-writer (:writer (:b-end t))\\\\n    60\\\\t   :mirror (atom (:dao.stream/cursor\\\\n    61\\\\t                   (stream/cursor (:reader (:b-end t)) :dao.stream/oldest)))})\\\\n    62\\\\t\\\\n    63\\\\t\\\\n    64\\\\t(defn- serve-mirror!\\\\n    65\\\\t  \\\\\\\"One mirror step over everything pending on the toy's request\\\\n    66\\\\t   buffer.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    67\\\\t  [peer]\\\\n    68\\\\t  (swap! (:mirror peer)\\\\n    69\\\\t         #(remote/mirror-step @(:table peer) (:b-reader peer) %\\\\n    70\\\\t                              (:b-writer peer))))\\\\n    71\\\\t\\\\n    72\\\\t\\\\n    73\\\\t(defn- server\\\\n    74\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The exporter's `serve!` (dao.stream.remote.md S2.3): assigns a\\\\n    75\\\\t   stable identity to `h`, entering it in the peer's table under\\\\n    76\\\\t   `chan`, if it is not already served.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    77\\\\t  [peer chan]\\\\n    78\\\\t  (fn [h]\\\\n    79\\\\t    (if-let [existing (some (fn [[id e]] (when (= h (:handle e)) id))\\\\n    80\\\\t                            @(:table peer))]\\\\n    81\\\\t      {:dao.stream/identity existing, :dao.stream/channel chan}\\\\n    82\\\\t      (let [id (str (gensym \\\\\\\"seg\\\\\\\"))]\\\\n    83\\\\t        (swap! (:table peer) assoc id {:handle h, :surface #{:reader}})\\\\n    84\\\\t        {:dao.stream/identity id, :dao.stream/channel chan}))))\\\\n    85\\\\t\\\\n    86\\\\t\\\\n    87\\\\t(defn- attacher\\\\n    88\\\\t  [t]\\\\n    89\\\\t  (remote/attacher\\\\n    90\\\\t    {:dao.stream.remote/channels {(:channel t) (:a-end t)}}))\\\\n    91\\\\t\\\\n    92\\\\t\\\\n    93\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n    94\\\\t;; The emitter side: a real machine parked through the engine\\\\n    95\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n    96\\\\t\\\\n    97\\\\t(def ^:private secret\\\\n    98\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The capability secret the test composition gives its machines.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    99\\\\t  \\\\\\\"yin.vm.ucf.remote-test/capability-secret\\\\\\\")\\\\n   100\\\\t\\\\n   101\\\\t\\\\n   102\\\\t(defn- make-ring-stream\\\\n   103\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The ring-buffer `:make-stream` this composition gives its\\\\n   104\\\\t   machines -- `yin.vm.test-utils`'s choice, at test scale.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   105\\\\t  [capacity]\\\\n   106\\\\t  (ringbuffer/create! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ringbuffer\\\\n   107\\\\t                       :dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity capacity}))\\\\n   108\\\\t\\\\n   109\\\\t\\\\n   110\\\\t(defn- new-machine\\\\n   111\\\\t  \\\\\\\"A fresh semantic machine over that composition.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   112\\\\t  []\\\\n   113\\\\t  (semantic/create-vm {:make-stream make-ring-stream\\\\n   114\\\\t                       :capability-secret secret}))\\\\n   115\\\\t\\\\n   116\\\\t\\\\n   117\\\\t(def ^:private load-ast\\\\n   118\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The trusted fresh path: the AST linearizer behind the code loader.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   119\\\\t  (vm/fresh-code-loader (linearize/ast-loader semantic/vm-load-program)\\\\n   120\\\\t                        vm/ast-contract))\\\\n   121\\\\t\\\\n   122\\\\t\\\\n   123\\\\t(defn- var-ref\\\\n   124\\\\t  [n]\\\\n   125\\\\t  {:type :variable, :name n})\\\\n   126\\\\t\\\\n   127\\\\t\\\\n   128\\\\t(defn- let1\\\\n   129\\\\t  \\\\\\\"`((fn [param] body) init)`.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   130\\\\t  [param init body]\\\\n   131\\\\t  {:type :application,\\\\n   132\\\\t   :operator {:type :lambda, :params [param], :body body},\\\\n   133\\\\t   :operands [init]})\\\\n   134\\\\t\\\\n   135\\\\t\\\\n   136\\\\t(defn- reader-datoms\\\\n   137\\\\t  \\\\\\\"Make a stream of `capacity`, mint a cursor on it, read one value.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   138\\\\t  [capacity]\\\\n   139\\\\t  (vm/ast->datoms\\\\n   140\\\\t    (let1 's {:type :stream/make, :buffer capacity}\\\\n   141\\\\t          {:type :stream/next,\\\\n   142\\\\t           :source {:type :stream/cursor, :source (var-ref 's)}})))\\\\n   143\\\\t\\\\n   144\\\\t\\\\n   145\\\\t(defn- parked-reader\\\\n   146\\\\t  \\\\\\\"A real machine parked through the engine on a `:stream-next` wait\\\\n   147\\\\t   over a fresh ring buffer: the code datoms, the parked VM, its\\\\n   148\\\\t   wait-set entry, and the stream handle the entry polls.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   149\\\\t  [capacity]\\\\n   150\\\\t  (let [datoms (reader-datoms capacity)\\\\n   151\\\\t        parked (vm/run (load-ast (new-machine) datoms))\\\\n   152\\\\t        entry (first (:wait-set parked))]\\\\n   153\\\\t    [datoms parked entry\\\\n   154\\\\t     (get (:resources parked) (:stream-id entry))]))\\\\n   155\\\\t\\\\n   156\\\\t\\\\n   157\\\\t(defn- registers-of\\\\n   158\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The register payload a parked entry carries.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   159\\\\t  [entry]\\\\n   160\\\\t  (select-keys entry [:segment :pc :env :stack :k]))\\\\n   161\\\\t\\\\n   162\\\\t\\\\n   163\\\\t(defn- quiesced-receiver\\\\n   164\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The fresh engine state a lower installs into: a real machine over\\\\n   165\\\\t   the same code image, quiesced, holding the lowered resource table\\\\n   166\\\\t   and the lowered wait-set entry -- nothing of the emitter's state\\\\n   167\\\\t   but the code image and the registers the entry carries.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   168\\\\t  [datoms lowered]\\\\n   169\\\\t  (assoc (load-ast (new-machine) datoms)\\\\n   170\\\\t         :control nil, :k nil,\\\\n   171\\\\t         :value :yin/blocked, :blocked? true, :halted? false,\\\\n   172\\\\t         :wait-set [(:entry lowered)],\\\\n   173\\\\t         :resources (:resources lowered)))\\\\n   174\\\\t\\\\n   175\\\\t\\\\n   176\\\\t(defn- cursor-entries\\\\n   177\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The cursor entries of a lowered resource table: maps keyed by the\\\\n   178\\\\t   UCF cell ids, each carrying its stream id and kept cursor.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   179\\\\t  [table]\\\\n   180\\\\t  (into {} (filter (fn [[_ v]] (and (map? v) (contains? v :stream-id))))\\\\n   181\\\\t        table))\\\\n   182\\\\t\\\\n   183\\\\t\\\\n   184\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n   185\\\\t;; The acceptance row: a real parked machine migrates and resumes\\\\n   186\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n   187\\\\t\\\\n   188\\\\t(deftest migrates-a-real-parked-machine-and-resumes\\\\n   189\\\\t  (let [t (toy)\\\\n   190\\\\t        peer (served-peer t)\\\\n   191\\\\t        serve! (server peer (:channel t))\\\\n   192\\\\t        attach! (attacher t)\\\\n   193\\\\t        [datoms parked entry src] (parked-reader 4)\\\\n   194\\\\t        cid (:id (:cursor-ref entry))\\\\n   195\\\\t        sid (:stream-id entry)\\\\n   196\\\\t        kept (get-in parked [:resources cid :cursor])\\\\n   197\\\\t        lifted (ucf.remote/lift-pending serve! (:resources parked) entry)\\\\n   198\\\\t        lowered (ucf.remote/lower-pending attach! (:yin.k/pending lifted)\\\\n   199\\\\t                                          (:yin.k/cells lifted)\\\\n   200\\\\t                                          (registers-of entry))\\\\n   201\\\\t        receiver (quiesced-receiver datoms lowered)]\\\\n   202\\\\t    (testing \\\\\\\"the machine parked through the engine\\\\\\\"\\\\n   203\\\\t      (is (vm/blocked? parked))\\\\n   204\\\\t      (is (= :next (:reason entry)))\\\\n   205\\\\t      (is (= :cursor-ref (get-in entry [:cursor-ref :type]))))\\\\n   206\\\\t    (testing \\\\\\\"the lift reads the engine's own wait-set entry\\\\\\\"\\\\n   207\\\\t      (is (= {:yin.k/reason :next, :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-0}\\\\n   208\\\\t             (:yin.k/pending lifted))\\\\n   209\\\\t          \\\\\\\"the cursor CELL id, S7.5.3 -- not a bare position\\\\\\\")\\\\n   210\\\\t      (is (= kept (get-in lifted [:yin.k/cells :yin.k/c-0 :yin.k/position]))\\\\n   211\\\\t          \\\\\\\"the cell carries the engine's kept cursor\\\\\\\")\\\\n   212\\\\t      (is (= #{:dao.stream.remote/v1}\\\\n   213\\\\t             (get-in lifted [:yin.k/requires :yin.k/cursor-profiles]))))\\\\n   214\\\\t    (testing \\\\\\\"the lower builds a fresh engine state\\\\\\\"\\\\n   215\\\\t      (let [e (:entry lowered)\\\\n   216\\\\t            rs (:resources lowered)\\\\n   217\\\\t            cid' (get-in e [:cursor-ref :id])\\\\n   218\\\\t            sid' (:stream-id e)]\\\\n   219\\\\t        (is (= (registers-of entry) (registers-of e))\\\\n   220\\\\t            \\\\\\\"the register context rides the lower verbatim\\\\\\\")\\\\n   221\\\\t        (is (not= cid cid'))\\\\n   222\\\\t        (is (not= sid sid'))\\\\n   223\\\\t        (is (not (contains? rs cid)))\\\\n   224\\\\t        (is (not (contains? rs sid)))\\\\n   225\\\\t        (is (= {:stream-id sid', :cursor kept} (get rs cid'))\\\\n   226\\\\t            \\\\\\\"one fresh cursor entry, at the kept position\\\\\\\")\\\\n   227\\\\t        (is (not= src (get rs sid'))\\\\n   228\\\\t            \\\\\\\"the stream entry is a reflection, not the emitter's handle\\\\\\\")\\\\n   229\\\\t        (is (= sid' (get-in rs [cid' :stream-id])))))\\\\n   230\\\\t    (testing \\\\\\\"the emitter's own wait resumes equivalently\\\\\\\"\\\\n   231\\\\t      (stream/append! src \\\\\\\"hello\\\\\\\")\\\\n   232\\\\t      (is (= \\\\\\\"hello\\\\\\\" (vm/value (vm/run parked)))))\\\\n   233\\\\t    (testing \\\\\\\"the migrated wait resumes in the fresh engine state\\\\\\\"\\\\n   234\\\\t      (is (vm/blocked? (vm/run receiver))\\\\n   235\\\\t          \\\\\\\"the reflection answers blocked until the mirror serves\\\\\\\")\\\\n   236\\\\t      (serve-mirror! peer)\\\\n   237\\\\t      (let [done (vm/run receiver)]\\\\n   238\\\\t        (is (vm/halted? done))\\\\n   239\\\\t        (is (= \\\\\\\"hello\\\\\\\" (vm/value done))\\\\n   240\\\\t            \\\\\\\"the source's own value, through the kept cursor\\\\\\\")\\\\n   241\\\\t        (is (empty? (:wait-set done)))))))\\\\n   242\\\\t\\\\n   243\\\\t\\\\n   244\\\\t(deftest forced-eviction-yields-gap-with-the-sources-cursor\\\\n   245\\\\t  (let [t (toy)\\\\n   246\\\\t        peer (served-peer t)\\\\n   247\\\\t        serve! (server peer (:channel t))\\\\n   248\\\\t        attach! (attacher t)\\\\n   249\\\\t        [datoms parked entry src] (parked-reader 2)\\\\n   250\\\\t        cid (:id (:cursor-ref entry))\\\\n   251\\\\t        kept (get-in parked [:resources cid :cursor])\\\\n   252\\\\t        lifted (ucf.remote/lift-pending serve! (:resources parked) entry)\\\\n   253\\\\t        lowered (ucf.remote/lower-pending attach! (:yin.k/pending lifted)\\\\n   254\\\\t                                          (:yin.k/cells lifted)\\\\n   255\\\\t                                          (registers-of entry))\\\\n   256\\\\t        cid' (get-in (:entry lowered) [:cursor-ref :id])\\\\n   257\\\\t        receiver (quiesced-receiver datoms lowered)]\\\\n   258\\\\t    (stream/append! src \\\\\\\"hello\\\\\\\")\\\\n   259\\\\t    (doseq [v [\\\\\\\"v1\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"v2\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"v3\\\\\\\"]]\\\\n   260\\\\t      (stream/append! src v))\\\\n   261\\\\t    (is (vm/blocked? (vm/run receiver)))\\\\n   262\\\\t    (serve-mirror! peer)\\\\n   263\\\\t    (let [expected (stream/next src kept)\\\\n   264\\\\t          done (vm/run receiver)]\\\\n   265\\\\t      (testing \\\\\\\"the program reads the source's own gap, verbatim\\\\\\\"\\\\n   266\\\\t        (is (= :dao.stream/gap (vm/value done)))\\\\n   267\\\\t        (is (= (:dao.stream/cursor expected)\\\\n   268\\\\t               (get-in done [:resources cid' :cursor]))\\\\n   269\\\\t            \\\\\\\"with the source's own recovery cursor in the cell\\\\\\\"))\\\\n   270\\\\t      (testing \\\\\\\"no replay and no silent skip: the walk continues\\\\n   271\\\\t                from the recovery cursor\\\\\\\"\\\\n   272\\\\t        (let [r (get-in done [:resources (:stream-id (:entry lowered))])\\\\n   273\\\\t              recovery (get-in done [:resources cid' :cursor])]\\\\n   274\\\\t          (is (= :dao.stream/blocked\\\\n   275\\\\t                 (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next r recovery))))\\\\n   276\\\\t          (serve-mirror! peer)\\\\n   277\\\\t          (let [ok (stream/next r recovery)]\\\\n   278\\\\t            (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome ok)))\\\\n   279\\\\t            (is (= \\\\\\\"v2\\\\\\\" (:dao.stream/value ok)))))))))\\\\n   280\\\\t\\\\n   281\\\\t\\\\n   282\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n   283\\\\t;; Unsatisfied: an unpublishable cursor, a source that cannot be\\\\n   284\\\\t;; served, a gone reflection\\\\n   285\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n   286\\\\t\\\\n   287\\\\t(defn- host-cursor-stream\\\\n   288\\\\t  \\\\\\\"A handle whose cursors hold a host object -- the case\\\\n   289\\\\t   dao.stream.remote.md S2.3 says cannot be served.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   290\\\\t  []\\\\n   291\\\\t  (reify stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\\\\n   292\\\\t    (descriptor\\\\n   293\\\\t      [_]\\\\n   294\\\\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\\\\n   295\\\\t       :dao.stream/identity \\\\\\\"host-cursor\\\\\\\"\\\\n   296\\\\t       :dao.stream/descriptor\\\\n   297\\\\t       {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream.test/channel\\\\n   298\\\\t        :dao.stream/identity \\\\\\\"host-cursor\\\\\\\"}})\\\\n   299\\\\t\\\\n   300\\\\t\\\\n   301\\\\t    stream/IDaoStreamReader\\\\n   302\\\\t\\\\n   303\\\\t    (cursor\\\\n   304\\\\t      [_ _]\\\\n   305\\\\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\\\\n   306\\\\t       :dao.stream/cursor {:position 0, :owner (fn [x] x)}})\\\\n   307\\\\t\\\\n   308\\\\t    (next\\\\n   309\\\\t      [_ _]\\\\n   310\\\\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/blocked})))\\\\n   311\\\\t\\\\n   312\\\\t\\\\n   313\\\\t(deftest a-cursor-that-cannot-round-trip-is-refused\\\\n   314\\\\t  (let [t (toy)\\\\n   315\\\\t        peer (served-peer t)\\\\n   316\\\\t        serve! (server peer (:channel t))\\\\n   317\\\\t        s (host-cursor-stream)\\\\n   318\\\\t        resources {:yin/host-stream s,\\\\n   319\\\\t                   :yin/host-cursor\\\\n   320\\\\t                   {:stream-id :yin/host-stream,\\\\n   321\\\\t                    :cursor {:position 0, :owner (fn [x] x)}}}\\\\n   322\\\\t        entry {:reason :next,\\\\n   323\\\\t               :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id :yin/host-cursor},\\\\n   324\\\\t               :stream-id :yin/host-stream}\\\\n   325\\\\t        refusal (ucf.remote/lift-pending serve! resources entry)]\\\\n   326\\\\t    (is (= :yin.k/unsatisfied (:yin.k/status refusal))\\\\n   327\\\\t        \\\\\\\"the profile claim is checked before anything is published\\\\\\\")\\\\n   328\\\\t    (is (= \\\\\\\"host-cursor\\\\\\\" (:dao.stream/identity refusal))\\\\n   329\\\\t        \\\\\\\"naming the stream identity\\\\\\\")\\\\n   330\\\\t    (is (empty? @(:table peer))\\\\n   331\\\\t        \\\\\\\"refused before `serve!` even mints the descriptor\\\\\\\")))\\\\n   332\\\\t\\\\n   333\\\\t\\\\n   334\\\\t(deftest lift-refuses-unsatisfied-when-the-source-cannot-be-served\\\\n   335\\\\t  (let [s (ring 4)]\\\\n   336\\\\t    (stream/append! s \\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\")\\\\n   337\\\\t    (let [c0 (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/cursor s :dao.stream/oldest))\\\\n   338\\\\t          resources {:yin/s s, :yin/c {:stream-id :yin/s, :cursor c0}}\\\\n   339\\\\t          entry {:reason :next,\\\\n   340\\\\t                 :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id :yin/c},\\\\n   341\\\\t                 :stream-id :yin/s}\\\\n   342\\\\t          refusal (ucf.remote/lift-pending (constantly nil) resources entry)]\\\\n   343\\\\t      (is (= :yin.k/unsatisfied (:yin.k/status refusal))\\\\n   344\\\\t          \\\\\\\"lift refuses before minting the value\\\\\\\")\\\\n   345\\\\t      (is (contains? refusal :dao.stream/identity)\\\\n   346\\\\t          \\\\\\\"naming the stream identity\\\\\\\"))))\\\\n   347\\\\t\\\\n   348\\\\t\\\\n   349\\\\t(deftest lower-refuses-unsatisfied-when-the-reflection-is-gone\\\\n   350\\\\t  (let [attach! (constantly {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\\\\n   351\\\\t                             :dao.stream.remote/reason\\\\n   352\\\\t                             :dao.stream.remote/not-found})\\\\n   353\\\\t        marker {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream,\\\\n   354\\\\t                :dao.stream/identity \\\\\\\"missing\\\\\\\",\\\\n   355\\\\t                :dao.stream/descriptor\\\\n   356\\\\t                {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote,\\\\n   357\\\\t                 :dao.stream/identity \\\\\\\"missing\\\\\\\",\\\\n   358\\\\t                 :dao.stream/channel\\\\n   359\\\\t                 {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream.test/channel,\\\\n   360\\\\t                  :dao.stream/identity \\\\\\\"elsewhere\\\\\\\"}}}\\\\n   361\\\\t        pending {:yin.k/reason :next, :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-0}\\\\n   362\\\\t        cells {:yin.k/c-0 {:yin.k/stream marker, :yin.k/position 0}}\\\\n   363\\\\t        lowered (ucf.remote/lower-pending attach! pending cells {})]\\\\n   364\\\\t    (is (= :yin.k/unsatisfied (:yin.k/status lowered)))\\\\n   365\\\\t    (is (= \\\\\\\"missing\\\\\\\" (:dao.stream/identity lowered))\\\\n   366\\\\t        \\\\\\\"naming the stream identity\\\\\\\")))\\\\n   367\\\\t\\\\n   368\\\\t\\\\n   369\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n   370\\\\t;; Frame-level cells (S7.5.3): shared and independent\\\\n   371\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n   372\\\\t\\\\n   373\\\\t(defn- stub-serve!\\\\n   374\\\\t  \\\\\\\"A `serve!` that assigns every handle to one identity per handle\\\\n   375\\\\t   under `chan`, with no wire underneath: these variants prove the\\\\n   376\\\\t   bridge's shape, the transport already proven above.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   377\\\\t  [chan]\\\\n   378\\\\t  (let [ids (atom {})]\\\\n   379\\\\t    (fn [h]\\\\n   380\\\\t      (let [id (or (get @ids h)\\\\n   381\\\\t                   (let [n (str (gensym \\\\\\\"seg\\\\\\\"))]\\\\n   382\\\\t                     (swap! ids assoc h n)\\\\n   383\\\\t                     n))]\\\\n   384\\\\t        {:dao.stream/identity id, :dao.stream/channel chan}))))\\\\n   385\\\\t\\\\n   386\\\\t\\\\n   387\\\\t(defn- stub-attach!\\\\n   388\\\\t  \\\\\\\"An `attach!` that answers ok at once with a reflection standing for\\\\n   389\\\\t   the descriptor's identity -- opaque here, since these variants\\\\n   390\\\\t   never poll it.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   391\\\\t  []\\\\n   392\\\\t  (fn [d]\\\\n   393\\\\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok,\\\\n   394\\\\t     :dao.stream/handle [:reflection-of (:dao.stream/identity d)]}))\\\\n   395\\\\t\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_19\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '125,150p;440,475p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | nl -ba -v125\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_19\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '125,150p;440,475p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | nl -ba -v125\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"   125\\\\t     exactly as an immediately-sent one does, so the entry is replaced by its\\\\n   126\\\\t     response reader and the machine stays blocked.\\\\n   127\\\\t   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\\\\n   128\\\\t     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it, checks correlation, and the\\\\n   129\\\\t     parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\\\\\\\"\\\\n   130\\\\t  ([base entry] (semantic-restore base entry (:value entry)))\\\\n   131\\\\t  ([base entry val]\\\\n   132\\\\t   (if (:request-sent entry)\\\\n   133\\\\t     (assoc base\\\\n   134\\\\t            :wait-set (conj (vec (or (:wait-set base) []))\\\\n   135\\\\t                            (response-wait-entry (:segment entry)\\\\n   136\\\\t                                                 (:pc entry)\\\\n   137\\\\t                                                 (:env entry)\\\\n   138\\\\t                                                 (:stack entry)\\\\n   139\\\\t                                                 (:k entry)\\\\n   140\\\\t                                                 (:call-id entry)))\\\\n   141\\\\t            :control nil\\\\n   142\\\\t            :k nil\\\\n   143\\\\t            :value :yin/blocked\\\\n   144\\\\t            :blocked? true\\\\n   145\\\\t            :halted? false)\\\\n   146\\\\t     (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\\\\n   147\\\\t           base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\\\\n   148\\\\t           val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)]\\\\n   149\\\\t       (put-registers base\\\\n   150\\\\t                      (:segment entry)\\\\n   151\\\\t                       parked (engine/park-continuation\\\\n   152\\\\t                                vm'\\\\n   153\\\\t                                {:segment seg, :pc (inc pc),\\\\n   154\\\\t                                 :env E, :stack St', :k K})\\\\n   155\\\\t                       call-id (get-in parked [:value :id])\\\\n   156\\\\t                       request (apply2/request call-id ffi-op args)\\\\n   157\\\\t                       result (apply2/put-request! call-in request)]\\\\n   158\\\\t                   (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\\\\n   159\\\\t                     :dao.stream/ok\\\\n   160\\\\t                     (-> parked\\\\n   161\\\\t                         (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\\\\n   162\\\\t                                 (response-wait-entry seg (inc pc) E St' K\\\\n   163\\\\t                                                      call-id))\\\\n   164\\\\t                         (assoc :control nil\\\\n   165\\\\t                                :k nil\\\\n   166\\\\t                                :value :yin/blocked\\\\n   167\\\\t                                :blocked? true\\\\n   168\\\\t                                :halted? false))\\\\n   169\\\\t                     :dao.stream/full\\\\n   170\\\\t                     (-> parked\\\\n   171\\\\t                         (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\\\\n   172\\\\t                                 {:segment seg, :pc (inc pc),\\\\n   173\\\\t                                  :env E, :stack St', :k K,\\\\n   174\\\\t                                  :request-sent true,\\\\n   175\\\\t                                  :call-id call-id,\\\\n   176\\\\t                                  :op ffi-op,\\\\n   177\\\\t                                  :reason :put,\\\\n   178\\\\t                                  :stream-id vm/call-in-stream-key,\\\\n   179\\\\t                                  :datom request})\\\\n   180\\\\t                         (assoc :control nil\\\\n   181\\\\t                                :k nil\\\\n   182\\\\t                                :value :yin/blocked\\\\n   183\\\\t                                :blocked? true\\\\n   184\\\\t                                :halted? false))\\\\n   185\\\\t                     (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"FFI request could not be appended\\\\\\\"\\\\n   186\\\\t                                     {:op ffi-op,\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_20\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '1158,1197p;1258,1290p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | nl -ba -v1158\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_20\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '1158,1197p;1258,1290p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | nl -ba -v1158\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"  1158\\\\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\\\\n  1159\\\\t      [state (assoc (error-refusal module-name e) :phase :loading)])))\\\\n  1160\\\\t\\\\n  1161\\\\t\\\\n  1162\\\\t(defn- settle\\\\n  1163\\\\t  \\\\\\\"Act on the response correlated with `entry`: a refusal (or loss) is\\\\n  1164\\\\t   raised as the effect's error; `:ok` runs step 5b against the live\\\\n  1165\\\\t   state and, discharged, starts the install child, the entry becoming\\\\n  1166\\\\t   an `:install` waiter. A module linked or installing meanwhile answers\\\\n  1167\\\\t   or joins instead, and a manifest naming another module than the one\\\\n  1168\\\\t   required is `:module-name-mismatch`, never installed. Returns `[state\\\\n  1169\\\\t   waiting woken]`.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  1170\\\\t  [state entry response]\\\\n  1171\\\\t  (let [state (retire state (:link-id entry) :restored)\\\\n  1172\\\\t        module-name (:name entry)\\\\n  1173\\\\t        waiter (-> entry\\\\n  1174\\\\t                   (dissoc :envelope :request :response :cursor :link-id)\\\\n  1175\\\\t                   (assoc :reason :install))]\\\\n  1176\\\\t    (cond\\\\n  1177\\\\t      (not= :ok (:status response))\\\\n  1178\\\\t      [state nil (refused-entry entry response)]\\\\n  1179\\\\t\\\\n  1180\\\\t      (some? (module/resolve-module (:modules state) module-name))\\\\n  1181\\\\t      [state nil (ready entry module-name)]\\\\n  1182\\\\t\\\\n  1183\\\\t      (contains? (:installs state) module-name) [state waiter nil]\\\\n  1184\\\\t\\\\n  1185\\\\t      ;; the linker checks the name (section 8.1); a response that names\\\\n  1186\\\\t      ;; another module is never installed under this one\\\\n  1187\\\\t      (not= module-name (get-in response [:manifest :yin.module/name]))\\\\n  1188\\\\t      [state nil\\\\n  1189\\\\t       (refused-entry entry\\\\n  1190\\\\t                      (linker/refused :module-name-mismatch\\\\n  1191\\\\t                                      {:requested module-name,\\\\n  1192\\\\t                                       :manifest (get-in\\\\n  1193\\\\t                                                   response\\\\n  1194\\\\t                                                   [:manifest\\\\n  1195\\\\t                                                    :yin.module/name])}))]\\\\n  1196\\\\t\\\\n  1197\\\\t      :else\\\\n  1198\\\\t  (let [[state waiting woken]\\\\n  1199\\\\t        (reduce (fn [[state waiting woken] entry]\\\\n  1200\\\\t                  (let [[state w k] (poll-link-entry state entry)]\\\\n  1201\\\\t                    [state\\\\n  1202\\\\t                     (cond-> waiting w (conj w))\\\\n  1203\\\\t                     (cond-> woken k (conj k))]))\\\\n  1204\\\\t                [state [] []]\\\\n  1205\\\\t                links)]\\\\n  1206\\\\t    (-> state\\\\n  1207\\\\t        (assoc :wait-set (into (vec others) waiting))\\\\n  1208\\\\t        (update :ready-queue (fnil into []) woken)\\\\n  1209\\\\t        advance-installs)))\\\\n  1210\\\\t\\\\n  1211\\\\t\\\\n  1212\\\\t(defn abandon-link\\\\n  1213\\\\t  \\\\\\\"A composition gives up on link `link-id` (section 7.2, step 7): the\\\\n  1214\\\\t   entry leaves the wait set and is restored with `reason` raised as the\\\\n  1215\\\\t   effect's error; the id is retired, so a response arriving later is\\\\n  1216\\\\t   `:late`. The linker side's `abandon` is the composition's call. An id\\\\n  1217\\\\t   no entry holds leaves the state unchanged.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  1218\\\\t  [state link-id reason]\\\\n  1219\\\\t  (let [held? (fn [e]\\\\n  1220\\\\t                (and (contains? #{:link-request :link-response} (:reason e))\\\\n  1221\\\\t                     (= link-id (:link-id e))))]\\\\n  1222\\\\t    (if-let [entry (first (filter held? (:wait-set state)))]\\\\n  1223\\\\t      (-> state\\\\n  1224\\\\t          (update :wait-set (fn [ws] (filterv (complement held?) ws)))\\\\n  1225\\\\t          (retire link-id :abandoned)\\\\n  1226\\\\t          (update :ready-queue (fnil conj [])\\\\n  1227\\\\t                  (refused-entry entry\\\\n  1228\\\\t                                 {:status :lost,\\\\n  1229\\\\t                                  :reason reason,\\\\n  1230\\\\t                                  :link-id link-id})))\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_21\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '940,962p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba -v940\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_21\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '940,962p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | nl -ba -v940\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"   940\\\\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\\\\n   941\\\\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\\\\n   942\\\\t\\\\n   943\\\\t**Cells lower into the private resource table (linker M4).** Specified by\\\\n   944\\\\t`yin.vm.linker.md` and implemented in M4. The lowering rule above -- each\\\\n   945\\\\tcell becomes a fresh store cursor entry -- is amended by the resource\\\\n   946\\\\tsplit of `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3: each cell becomes a cursor entry in the\\\\n   947\\\\treceiver's private `:resources` table, seeded with its carried position,\\\\n   948\\\\tand every `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one\\\\n   949\\\\tcell still share one entry and one ref per cell keeps its own, but no\\\\n   950\\\\tstore key is ever created, and program values keep only the opaque\\\\n   951\\\\treference ids, exactly as a running program holds them.\\\\n   952\\\\t\\\\n   953\\\\tA stream reference is portable when the exporter can enter its handle in a\\\\n   954\\\\t`dao.stream.remote.md` table and publish a remote descriptor for the\\\\n   955\\\\toriginal logical stream. This applies to local implementations,\\\\n   956\\\\tincluding an in-memory or string-backed stream; they need no native\\\\n   957\\\\tnetwork transport. The exporter checks the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor\\\\n   958\\\\tprofile before encoding the cell, and the receiver holds a reflection\\\\n   959\\\\tafter `attach!`. The mirror preserves the source's cursor and outcome\\\\n   960\\\\tsemantics, including `gap`; it does not turn `:oldest` or `:newest` into\\\\n   961\\\\ta kept position. A raw host handle\\\\n   962\\\\tstill never crosses the value boundary. A stream that cannot be served or\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_22\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-27 17:29:05 GMT\\\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-28 00:29:05 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\\\n\\\\n**Slice 8 verdict: request changes.** The two files contain the reported real-entry lift, codec round-trip check, and frame-wide cell sharing. The supplied 16-test/106-assertion result is green, but two lowered waits cannot resume correctly through the current engine:\\\\n\\\\n- **Link waits use keys the engine does not read.** [remote.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:535) lowers the link pair under identity-derived resource IDs. The engine polls the response at the fixed `module/link-response-resource` key ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1215)), and request retry uses the fixed `link-request-resource` key ([module.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:403)). The link round-trip tests check fields and resource presence, but never poll a lowered link wait ([remote_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc:604)).\\\\n- **A retained FFI request loses its response route.** [remote.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:292) lifts only the request stream and creates no response cell; [remote.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:520) lowers that request accordingly. After the retry succeeds, `semantic-restore` creates a response wait on the receiver’s fixed local call-out keys ([semantic.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:132)). A response to the migrated request arrives on the emitter’s pair, so that wait is stranded. The test checks the rebuilt envelope but does not resume the retry ([remote_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc:577)).\\\\n\\\\nI did not edit files or rerun suites. The reported full-lane slice-5 and slice-peer failures are outside this gate.\\\\n\\\\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\\\\nSign-off: DENIED\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"turn.completed\\\",\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":290052,\\\"cached_input_tokens\\\":245120,\\\"cache_write_input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":1851,\\\"reasoning_output_tokens\\\":85}}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-stream/1791400000000-reviewer-stream-s3b-rereview.codex.findings.md:96:This pass did not rerun the full repository lanes, all process-level main tests, or slow process tests. The engineer's reported full-lane missing `handoff-v2.txt` baseline failure and earlier slow-test success are reported evidence, not newly certified results. The focused runs independently establish that the remediations and their affected portable tests pass on JVM, Node and Dart, including the real-wire JVM test and renamed Node adapter test.\ncollab/dao-stream/1789014225034-stream-jing-remote-phase2-r2.glm-5.3.stdout.log:19:| lane | result | r1 baseline |\ncollab/dao-stream/1789280260256-review-macro-stream-process-r5.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '195,375p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '540,605p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '805,865p'; tail -35 docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   195\\t\\n   196\\t`ctx` is the expander's process state and is threaded through every call:\\n   197\\t\\n   198\\t```\\n   199\\t{:store    {sym lambda-ast}       ; macro environment, persisted across batches\\n   200\\t :alloc    {:next-eid n}          ; monotonic negative counter, never re-seeded upward\\n   201\\t :guards   {:max-depth 100  :max-nodes-per-expansion 10000\\n   202\\t            :max-steps 100000  :max-datoms-per-batch nil}\\n   203\\t :eval     (fn [lambda-ast env prelude budget] → value)   ; §3.3\\n   204\\t :t        0}                     ; batch counter; incremented once per expand-batch\\n   205\\t```\\n   206\\t\\n   207\\t`expand-batch`:\\n   208\\t\\n   209\\t1. **Admit.** The batch is indexed with `index-datoms` and checked as a\\n   210\\t   whole — **every entity in the index, not only those reachable from the\\n   211\\t   root**, because harvest (step 2) reads disconnected definitions: every\\n   212\\t   `:yin/type` is in the closed vocabulary (§3.3), every ref resolves to an\\n   213\\t   entity in the index, the whole graph is acyclic, every ground value is\\n   214\\t   plain data, and every `:macro? true` lambda is the value operand of a\\n   215\\t   `(yin/def <literal sym> …)` — a macro lambda anywhere else has no meaning\\n   216\\t   on either side of the boundary and, forwarded, would load on the walker\\n   217\\t   as a plain closure, the quiet case decision 5 exists to prevent — all\\n   218\\t   *before* any recursive decoding (Appendix B). An in-memory medium can\\n   219\\t   carry host values, and the sandbox's closed-input guarantee holds only if\\n   220\\t   nothing else is admitted. A failure here is\\n   221\\t   `{:status :error :error {:kind :malformed-input :reason :unknown-type |\\n   222\\t   :dangling-ref | :cyclic | :host-value | :stray-macro-lambda :eid e}}`\\n   223\\t   carrying a **batch-level\\n   224\\t   event** (§4.1) — allocated from the watermark alone, since the index may\\n   225\\t   be unusable — and the advanced `:ctx`. Diagnostics name entities, never\\n   226\\t   carry the offending values.\\n   227\\t2. **Harvest.** `definitions` finds every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the\\n   228\\t   batch, in batch order, last wins: `v` a `:macro? true` lambda →\\n   229\\t   `assoc` into `:store`; `v` anything else (a plain lambda, a variable, a\\n   230\\t   literal) → `dissoc`, so a redefinition as a non-macro stops expansion of\\n   231\\t   that name. Harvest is *syntactic* and whole-batch by policy: a definition\\n   232\\t   in an unexecuted branch, or after its first use in the same batch, is\\n   233\\t   still in force for the whole batch. (Clojure defines a macro only when the\\n   234\\t   `defmacro` form executes; this design does not, and says so.)\\n   235\\t3. **Replace.** Each macro definition node is replaced by\\n   236\\t   `{:type :literal :value <sym>}`, so a batch whose root is a definition\\n   237\\t   still forwards a complete program and a REPL prints the name. Plain\\n   238\\t   `yin/def`s are forwarded untouched.\\n   239\\t4. **Expand.** `expand-node` (§3.2) runs from the root over the index.\\n   240\\t5. **Post-harvest.** Expansion can *generate* definitions that only take\\n   241\\t   the `yin/def` shape after it — `(defn m …)` above all. The store for the\\n   242\\t   **next** batch is recomputed by one last-wins pass over the union of (a)\\n   243\\t   the source definitions step 2 saw, at their batch positions, and (b) every\\n   244\\t   `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the final tree that was emitted from a\\n   245\\t   macro's output — identified by `m` = an expansion event on its log copy,\\n   246\\t   never by eid, since re-parented copies of source nodes also have fresh\\n   247\\t   eids — at the batch position of the call site it came from. A macro\\n   248\\t   lambda `assoc`es, anything else `dissoc`es; the last definition of a\\n   249\\t   name in that order wins. **Ordering rule:** step 2's source-only store\\n   250\\t   governs the whole current batch; step 5's result governs from the next\\n   251\\t   batch. Consequences, stated: `(def m f)` then `(defmacro m …)` keeps the\\n   252\\t   macro (the later source definition wins, and the surviving plain\\n   253\\t   `yin/def` in the tree is a *source* node step 2 already ranked, not a\\n   254\\t   generated one); `(defmacro m …)` then `(defn m …)` removes it (the\\n   255\\t   generated plain definition is later); within one batch a macro `m` and a\\n   256\\t   `(defn m …)` both apply to their own forms in source order, as Clojure\\n   257\\t   would not, and the design accepts that one-batch divergence in exchange\\n   258\\t   for a one-pass, deterministic rule.\\n   259\\t6. **Mark tail.** `mark-tail` recomputes `:tail?` over the whole final tree\\n   260\\t   (§3.4).\\n   261\\t7. **Emit.** Output is emitted bottom-up as a self-contained batch with a\\n   262\\t   fresh `:yin/root` fact; the log copy carries `m` per §4.1.\\n   263\\t8. **Scan.** A final walk asserts no lambda with `:yin/macro? true` remains.\\n   264\\t   With admission rejecting stray macro lambdas, step 3 replacing source\\n   265\\t   definitions, and `:generated-macro` rejecting generated ones, this cannot\\n   266\\t   fire; it is the guard for decision 5 and a violation is a forwarder\\n   267\\t   defect, thrown as `{:kind :scan-failed :eid e}`.\\n   268\\t\\n   269\\tAn `:error` result carries the event (§4.1) in `:log` and the advanced\\n   270\\t`:ctx`; `:datoms` is absent. `expand` unwraps `:ok` and throws on `:error`\\n   271\\tfor callers that want the exception. Same inputs, same output, on every\\n   272\\thost.\\n   273\\t\\n   274\\t**Working representation.** Traversal, cycle detection, and shadow tracking\\n   275\\trun over *eids in the index*. Plain AST maps are materialised only at the\\n   276\\tboundary of one body invocation — operands decoded for `invoke`, the returned\\n   277\\tvalue validated and re-encoded to datoms with fresh ids, and re-expansion\\n   278\\tcontinues over the index of the batch-so-far. Internal identity never\\n   279\\tappears in the map representation macros see (`valid-ast?` rejects `:eid`).\\n   280\\t\\n   281\\t**Allocation.** `:alloc` is seeded per batch to\\n   282\\t`(min (:next-eid alloc) (dec min-eid-in-batch) (- (inc datom/first-user-id)))`\\n   283\\t— the incoming watermark is a term, so the counter only ever decreases across\\n   284\\ta session and event eids on the accumulated log never collide. It is returned\\n   285\\tadvanced in both `:ok` and `:error` results; a retry of a staged flush reuses\\n   286\\tthe staged allocation and never allocates again.\\n   287\\t\\n   288\\t### 3.2 The algorithm\\n   289\\t\\n   290\\tTraversal visits ref attributes in a **fixed order** so fresh ids and\\n   291\\tgensyms are deterministic across hosts: `:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`\\n   292\\t(left to right), `:yin/test`, `:yin/consequent`, `:yin/alternate`,\\n   293\\t`:yin/body`, `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node`, `:yin/source`. `shadow` is the\\n   294\\tset of names bound by enclosing `:lambda :params`.\\n   295\\t\\n   296\\t```\\n   297\\tmacro-of ctx eid shadow :\\n   298\\t  case operator(eid)\\n   299\\t    :variable v  → v ∉ shadow ∧ (:store ctx) v\\n   300\\t    :lambda  l   → l when (:macro? l)\\n   301\\t    _            → nil\\n   302\\t\\n   303\\texpand-node ctx eid shadow depth :\\n   304\\t  if type(eid) = :application ∧ (m = macro-of ctx eid shadow):\\n   305\\t      ev        = fresh-event ctx eid m                  ; allocated first: every failure below is recorded on ev\\n   306\\t      if depth ≥ :max-depth: fail ev {:kind :depth-guard :depth :chain}\\n   307\\t      operands  = [decode c | c ∈ operands(eid)]         ; unexpanded AST maps\\n   308\\t      out       = invoke m operands ctx                  ; §3.3 — arity, fuel, suspension fail on ev\\n   309\\t      check valid-ast? out {:allow-macro-lambda? false}  ; node count ≤ :max-nodes-per-expansion\\n   310\\t      root'     = encode out (fresh ids from :alloc)     ; joins the index of the batch-so-far\\n   311\\t      record ev :yin/expansion-root root'\\n   312\\t      return expand-node ctx root' shadow (depth+1)      ; re-expand the output under the call site's scope\\n   313\\t  else if type(eid) = :application:\\n   314\\t      op'       = expand-node ctx operator(eid) shadow depth        ; operator FIRST\\n   315\\t      eid'      = if op' = operator(eid) then eid else copy eid with operator op'\\n   316\\t      if macro-of ctx eid' shadow:\\n   317\\t          return expand-node ctx eid' shadow depth        ; became a macro call: takes the first arm with the ORIGINAL operands\\n   318\\t      operands' = [expand-node ctx c shadow depth | c ∈ operands(eid)]\\n   319\\t      return if operands' = operands(eid) then eid' else copy eid' with operands'\\n   320\\t  else:\\n   321\\t      shadow'   = if type(eid) = :lambda then shadow ∪ params(eid) else shadow\\n   322\\t      children' = [expand-node ctx c shadow' depth | c ∈ children(eid) in fixed order]\\n   323\\t      return if children' = children(eid) then eid else copy eid with children'\\n   324\\t```\\n   325\\t\\n   326\\t`fail ev …` writes `:yin/error` on the event and unwinds to `expand-batch`,\\n   327\\twhich returns the `:error` result with that event in `:log`. Cycle and\\n   328\\tdangling-ref detection happened at admission (§3.1 step 1), so this walk is\\n   329\\tover a known-acyclic graph; output encoded from validated plain maps is\\n   330\\tacyclic by construction.\\n   331\\t\\n   332\\tThe application branch expands the **operator before the operands** so that\\n   333\\tan operator which rewrites into a macro name hands that macro its operands\\n   334\\t*as written*: in `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with `choose` → `defn`, `defn`\\n   335\\tmust receive `(x 1)` unexpanded so the lambda it builds can shadow `x`.\\n   336\\tExpanding operands first would violate outermost-first for exactly the\\n   337\\tmacros (binders) that depend on it. Recognition is not an expansion — the\\n   338\\tre-entry is at the same depth — and a successful re-check goes straight into\\n   339\\tthe invocation arm, which re-expands at `depth+1`, so a chain of generated\\n   340\\tapplications consumes depth and cannot rewrite forever at constant depth.\\n   341\\t\\n   342\\tProperties, each a Phase 1 test:\\n   343\\t\\n   344\\t- **Outermost first.** A macro sees its operands as written. This is what\\n   345\\t  makes `->`-style macros expressible and what retires the shadow hints:\\n   346\\t  in `(defn f [x] (x 1))`, `defn` expands *before* its operands are examined,\\n   347\\t  yielding `(yin/def f (lambda [x] (x 1)))`; re-expansion then meets `(x 1)`\\n   348\\t  under `:lambda [x]`, so `x` is shadowed and left alone even if a macro\\n   349\\t  named `x` exists. yang's frontend hint existed only because the frontend\\n   350\\t  had to decide this before expansion.\\n   351\\t- **Fixpoint by construction.** Every node reachable from the final root\\n   352\\t  was visited after its last rewrite, *including* an application whose\\n   353\\t  operator was rewritten into a macro name by a child expansion\\n   354\\t  (`((choose) x)` where `choose` expands to `m`): the application branch\\n   355\\t  re-checks after expanding the operator and before touching the operands.\\n   356\\t- **Depth** counts nesting of expansion inside expansion output. Direct\\n   357\\t  recursion without a base case fails at 100 with\\n   358\\t  `{:kind :depth-guard :depth 100 :chain [eid ...]}`. Siblings do not\\n   359\\t  accumulate depth.\\n   360\\t- **Shadowing is lexical and complete.** `let`, `do`, and every binder every\\n   361\\t  frontend emits lower to `:lambda :params`; `yin/def` is global and is the\\n   362\\t  store. There is no other binder in the Universal AST. The shadow set is\\n   363\\t  extended *at the lambda node*, so an operator-position lambda's params\\n   364\\t  shadow its body and not its sibling operands.\\n   365\\t- **Generated macro lambdas are rejected in v1 — wherever they appear.** A\\n   366\\t  macro whose output contains a `:macro? true` lambda, as a definition value\\n   367\\t  *or* inline in operator position, fails `{:kind :invalid-output :reason\\n   368\\t  :generated-macro}`; `valid-ast?` is called with `:allow-macro-lambda?\\n   369\\t  false` on expansion output. `macro-of`'s inline-lambda case therefore only\\n   370\\t  ever fires on lambdas the *source* batch contained. Macro-defining macros\\n   371\\t  and generated inline macros are reserved together (§7); admitting the\\n   372\\t  second without the first would need a rule for keeping a generated macro\\n   373\\t  body as transformer syntax through re-expansion, which is the same\\n   374\\t  problem.\\n   375\\t- **Failure leaves a record.** The attempt event is allocated before the\\n   540\\t\\n   541\\t## 4. Provenance\\n   542\\t\\n   543\\t### 4.1 The expansion event\\n   544\\t\\n   545\\tEvery attempt, successful or not, produces one event; so does every batch\\n   546\\trejected at admission. Events and the `m`-tagged output are appended to the\\n   547\\t**log** medium, when the expander has one; `program-out` receives only\\n   548\\tprogram.\\n   549\\t\\n   550\\t```\\n   551\\t[ev :yin/type           :macro-expand-event  t default-op]\\n   552\\t[ev :yin/source-batch   <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; which source batch; always present\\n   553\\t[ev :yin/source-call    <call-eid>           t default-op]   ; eid in that batch, or in the log copy of the enclosing expansion; absent on an admission failure\\n   554\\t[ev :yin/macro-name     <sym>                t default-op]   ; when the operator was a :variable; absent for an inline macro lambda or an admission failure\\n   555\\t[ev :yin/macro          <lambda-eid>         t default-op]   ; only when durable (§4.2)\\n   556\\t[ev :yin/expansion-root <root'>              t default-op]   ; on success; names the LOG copy\\n   557\\t[ev :yin/error          {:kind ...}          t default-op]   ; on failure; plain data naming entities, never carrying input values\\n   558\\t[ev :yin/timestamp      <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; logical, never a host clock\\n   559\\t```\\n   560\\t\\n   561\\tAn **admission-failure event** is the same entity with only `:yin/type`,\\n   562\\t`:yin/source-batch`, `:yin/error`, and `:yin/timestamp`: the batch had no\\n   563\\tusable call or macro to name. It is allocated from `:alloc`'s watermark\\n   564\\t(which needs no index), so a corrupt batch still produces exactly one\\n   565\\tplain-data event, advances `:ctx` once, and is retried through the same\\n   566\\tper-medium staging as any other log payload — never regenerated.\\n   567\\t\\n   568\\tThe log copy of expansion output carries `m = ev`; log copies of unchanged\\n   569\\toperands and ancestors carry `m = default-op` (re-parenting, not macro\\n   570\\toutput). **`program-out` datoms all carry `default-op`**: `m = ev` would be\\n   571\\ta cross-medium ref (§4.2). A nested expansion's `:yin/source-call` names a\\n   572\\tnode in the log copy of the enclosing expansion's output, which is how the\\n   573\\tchain source → event → root → event → root is walked for generated syntax.\\n   574\\t\\\"Every datom this expansion produced\\\" is `[?d _ _ _ ?ev]` on the log.\\n   575\\t\\n   576\\t`yin.vm.macro/event-schema` declares these attributes (`:yin/source-call`,\\n   577\\t`:yin/macro`, `:yin/expansion-root` as refs) for compositions that commit a\\n   578\\tlog to `dao.space`; the transactor relocates only declared refs, so a\\n   579\\tcomposition merges this fragment with `yin.vm/schema` before committing.\\n   580\\t\\n   581\\t### 4.2 Identity across media\\n   582\\t\\n   583\\t`program-in`, `program-out`, and the log are different media. A ref written\\n   584\\ton one that names an entity on another is meaningful only when the named\\n   585\\tid is **durable** — a permanent positive id from a committed stream. The\\n   586\\ttransactor (`dao.space.transact`) resolves tempids within one transaction;\\n   587\\tcommitting two batches separately does not connect them. Therefore:\\n   588\\t\\n   589\\t- `program-out` batches are self-contained: unchanged subtrees are copied,\\n   590\\t  with fresh ids, not referenced (decision 8).\\n   591\\t- `:yin/source-call` on an event names the call's eid *as it appeared in\\n   592\\t  the source batch*, qualified by `:yin/source-batch`; it is descriptive\\n   593\\t  until both media are committed under one resolution. `:yin/macro` is\\n   594\\t  written only when the macro's id is durable; otherwise `:yin/macro-name`\\n   595\\t  carries the name. Names are metadata, never identity repair.\\n   596\\t- `:yin/expansion-root` names the **log copy** of the output. The executed\\n   597\\t  program on `program-out` is a distinct copy with distinct ids; this design\\n   598\\t  promises no id-level correspondence between them. A composition that needs\\n   599\\t  one commits program and log in one transaction (they are one batch to the\\n   600\\t  transactor) or records the mapping itself.\\n   601\\t- A pipeline that wants queryable provenance commits `program-in` first and\\n   602\\t  expands against durable ids — the ordering rule of\\n   603\\t  `cross-language-macro.md` §4, retained. `ADR 0003` makes `dao.space` the\\n   604\\t  medium for that commit.\\n   605\\t\\n   805\\tnative lowering; definition consumed and replaced by a name literal; a\\n   806\\tdefinition in an unexecuted branch is harvested; last definition wins;\\n   807\\t**redefinition: `(defmacro m)` then source `(def m f)` removes the macro\\n   808\\tin-batch; `(def m f)` then `(defmacro m)` keeps it next batch; `(defmacro\\n   809\\tm)` then `(defn m …)` removes it next batch; an existing macro and a\\n   810\\tseparate `(defn m …)` batch removes it next batch; each repeated with the\\n   811\\tdefinition node re-parented (fresh eid) by an unrelated expansion in the\\n   812\\tsame batch**; a bare `:macro? true` lambda outside a definition fails\\n   813\\tadmission as `:stray-macro-lambda`;\\n   814\\toutermost-first (`->`-style macro); shadowing by `let`, `fn`, and by a\\n   815\\t`defn`'s own params without any frontend hint; `(fn [m] (m 1))` with `m` a\\n   816\\tmacro is left intact; a macro name in operand position is left as a\\n   817\\tvariable; **an operator macro returning a macro name is expanded with its\\n   818\\toperands unexpanded (`((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with a macro `x` in scope\\n   819\\tyields a lambda whose body still says `(x 1)`), and an operator-selected\\n   820\\tmacro that discards an operand containing a failing macro call succeeds**;\\n   821\\ta macro emitting a macro lambda — as a definition or inline — fails\\n   822\\t`:generated-macro`; depth guard with chain; per-expansion bound from node\\n   823\\tcount before emission; per-batch bound; step budget on an infinite body;\\n   824\\tparked body rejected; **cyclic operand, dangling ref, unknown node type, a\\n   825\\thost value in a literal, and a cycle inside a disconnected definition all\\n   826\\tfail at admission before any decode, each producing one admission event\\n   827\\tthat survives a log `full`**; invalid output including a host function inside a\\n   828\\tliteral and an `:eid` key; arity and `&`; **gensyms differ across two\\n   829\\tbatches with overlapping tempids when ctx is threaded, and are equal on\\n   830\\treplay of the same ctx**; a stream effect in a body fails with the\\n   831\\tconstructor error; `mark-tail` on `if`/`let`/lambda spines **and clears a\\n   832\\tstale flag on marked syntax moved into an operand and into an `if` test**;\\n   833\\ta macro-free deeply nested batch passes; determinism (two runs, equal\\n   834\\tvectors); macro-free batch returned equal modulo the root fact; a\\n   835\\tcorpus-scale batch at 80% of a configured per-batch bound passes; the\\n   836\\tforwarder driven through `observer/run-on-stream`: `out=ok, log=full`\\n   837\\tretries only the log; `out=full` retries only the program; repeated `full`;\\n   838\\tno log medium; **an expansion failure advances the cursor, stages only the\\n   839\\tevent, and appears in `drain-errors`; a failed batch then a successful one\\n   840\\tin a single `run-on-stream` call yields `:forwarded 1` and one error; a\\n   841\\tdrained expander reports nothing on the next drain**; a forwarder-defect\\n   842\\tthrow does not advance the cursor.\\n   843\\t\\n   844\\t### Phase 2 — REPL composition\\n   845\\t\\n   846\\tDeliverables per §6.1. Tests: define in one input, use in the next; use\\n   847\\tfrom `:python` after defining in `:clojure`; `(reset)` forgets; `(compile)`\\n   848\\trenders four stages; a runtime error from a macro-named variable in operand\\n   849\\tposition is an unbound-variable error, not a call; a bad macro input prints\\n   850\\tits error and the *next* input evaluates normally (no head-of-line block);\\n   851\\t`repl-state` lists the store's macro names.\\n   852\\t\\n   853\\tDeferred to the semantic VM's own Phases 1–2 (`yin.vm.semantic` and\\n   854\\t`linearize` do not exist yet): the `:semantic` VM type runs the same session\\n   855\\twith equal values, and a tail-position macro in a 10⁵-iteration loop keeps\\n   856\\tcontinuation depth 0 — measured by inspecting `k`, since equal values alone\\n   857\\tdo not prove it.\\n   858\\t\\n   859\\t### Phase 3 — Cross-language verification\\n   860\\t\\n   861\\tTests (`python_macro_test.clj`, `php_macro_test.clj`): a scalar-signature\\n   862\\tClojure macro `(defmacro twice [f] (yin/application f [(yin/application f\\n   863\\t[(yin/literal 1)])]))` called as `twice(inc_fn)` from Python and\\n   864\\t`twice($inc_fn)` from PHP; `yin/name-of` with a Python string, a Clojure\\n   865\\tsymbol, and a `:variable`; a PHP `for`-loop variable shadowing a macro name;\\n| 8 | Final scan must be scope-aware (astra P2-6, glm P3-8) | §3.1 step 8 scans only for `:macro?` lambdas; application fixpoint is by construction via #4 |\\n| 9 | Tail marking must clear stale flags; lambda rule ambiguous (astra P2-7) | §3.4 whole-tree recompute with per-node context rules; lambda body always tail; `linearize` owns inlining |\\n| 10 | Provenance identity: same tempid in two batches, generated syntax, program↔log correspondence, event schema home, anonymous macros (astra P2-8) | §4.1 `:yin/source-batch`; nested `source-call` into the log copy; `expansion-root` names the log copy, correspondence not promised; `macro/event-schema`; `:yin/macro-name` absent for inline lambdas |\\n| 11 | Output validation alone does not close the sandbox input (astra P2-9) | §3.1 step 1 admission validates ground values; §3.3 validates operands going in; `:eval`/prelude trust contract stated; fuel and payload limits recorded as known v1 limits |\\n| 12 | Cycle detection after recursive decode overflows; `eid(node)` undefined on maps (astra P2-10, glm P3-8) | §3.1 admission over the index; working-representation paragraph; Appendix B |\\n| 13 | \\\"As in Clojure\\\" is wrong for syntactic harvest; \\\"helpers are other macros\\\" unsupported; macro/runtime namespace relation unstated (astra P3-11) | §3.1 policy stated on its own terms; §3.3 helpers are prelude only, body expansion reserved; §2.2 two namespaces |\\n| 14 | `program-out` `m = ev` would be a cross-medium ref; event attrs dropped from schema but still emitted (glm P3-5) | Decision 9 and §4.1: `program-out` carries `default-op`; `macro/event-schema` |\\n| 15 | Root fact unconditional vs \\\"compile output unchanged\\\" (glm P3-6) | Phase 0 test reworded \\\"modulo the root fact\\\" |\\n| 16 | Phase 2 `:semantic` test has an unstated prerequisite (glm P3-7) | Phase 2 defers it to the semantic spec's Phases 1–2, with the continuation-depth measurement |\\n| 17 | Missed-macro diagnostics (glm P3-9) | §6.1 `repl-state` lists the store's names |\\n| — | Qualify \\\"never holds VM control state\\\" (astra, layering) | §1.2 table: the *program* evaluator's state |\\n| — | Later batch ≠ expansion inside a loaded continuation (astra P3-11) | Appendix A #6 already states the limit; decision 11 makes the consumed-batch semantics explicit |\\n\\nRound 3 (`collab/1789279450960-review-macro-stream-process-r3.{gpt-6-astra,glm-5.3}.findings.md`), on revision 3:\\n\\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 18 | Re-check happens after operands expanded; a newly discovered binder macro gets rewritten operands (astra P1-1) | §3.2 application branch: operator first, re-check, then operands; test with `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` |\\n| 19 | `(defn m …)` after `(defmacro m …)` not caught — the `yin/def` shape appears only post-expansion; non-lambda redefinitions ignored (astra P2-2) | §3.1 step 2 `dissoc`es on any non-macro value; new step 5 post-harvest over the final tree, effective next batch; ordering rule stated |\\n| 20 | Inline generated macro lambda both required by a test and forbidden by the validator (astra P2-3) | Prohibition kept and widened to `:generated-macro`; the test case removed; both reserved together |\\n| 21 | `:errors` never drained; a stale error suppresses later evaluation; A-ok/B-error in one call skips A (astra P2-4, glm P2-1) | §5 `drain-errors` read-and-reset with `:forwarded`; §6.1 drives the evaluator on `:forwarded > 0` independently of `:errors` |\\n| 22 | Admission checks only root-reachable, harvest decodes the whole batch (astra P2-5) | §3.1 step 1 checks every entity in the index |\\n| 23 | Admission failures have no event path (astra P2-6) | §4.1 admission-failure event allocated from the watermark, no input values in diagnostics |\\n| 24 | `run-on-stream` error shape: a value-shaped error lets un-updated callers continue silently (glm P3-2); load vs flush failure must carry different cursors (astra) | §5 prerequisite: keep the throw, carry `:session` in `ex-data`, cursor position depends on where the failure was; Phase 0 tests |\\n| 25 | Admission should check the closed node vocabulary (glm P3-3) | §3.1 step 1 `:unknown-type` |\\n| 26 | `closed`/`invalid-value`/`transport-error` are protocol outcomes, not defects; `full` may be permanent (astra) | §5 table reworded; outcome and both slots preserved in the throw |\\n| 27 | Decision 5 \\\"unbound variable\\\" too strong given §2.2; decisions 10/11 out of order; §3.4 exactness under the no-inline contract (astra, glm) | Decision 5 qualified; order fixed; §3.4 sentence added |\\n\\nRound 4 (`collab/1789279972076-review-macro-stream-process-r4.{gpt-6-astra,glm-5.3}.findings.md`), on revision 4; glm approved r4:\\n\\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 28 | Post-harvest removes a later winning source macro because an earlier plain source `yin/def` survives in the tree (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 is a unified last-wins over source definitions at their positions and *generated* definitions (identified by `m` = event, never eid) at their call sites' positions; consequences enumerated; four acceptance cases plus re-parented variants in Phase 1 |\\n| 29 | Stray source macro lambda outside a definition shape has no disposition (glm P3-1) | §3.1 step 1 `:stray-macro-lambda` at admission; step 8 named `:scan-failed` as a forwarder defect |\\n| 30 | Appendix C row 5 names the retired `:generated-definition` (glm P3-2) | Row 5 annotated |\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-stream/1792495500000-engineer-codec-c2.claude-sonnet-5-5.prompt.md:42:   - Clean corpora and baseline exceptions tests pass.\ncollab/dao-stream/1790585138891-reviewer-one-envelope-slice1-gate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:6:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md collab/1790577198000-vm-engineer-one-envelope-slice1.prompt.md collab/1790577198000-vm-engineer-one-envelope-slice1.claude-opus-5-5.report.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-28 06:03:19 GMT  \\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-28 13:03:19 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\n\\n## Ruling\\n\\nConverge **the application wire vocabulary** on `dao.stream.apply`. Keep `dao.stream.apply` as the open envelope contract and `dao.stream.rpc` as the client driver. This is a small breaking change while the rpc vocabulary is newly landed and gated; a compatibility shim would leave two accepted wire languages and prolong the drift this ruling is meant to end.\\n\\nThere are two corrections to the proposed direction. First, the present rpc client has random safe ID allocation and tracks outstanding requests, but it has **no numeric cap on the outstanding map**: `request!` allocates whenever there is no retained unsent request, and acceptance adds an entry to `:outstanding` without a size check ([rpc.cljc:212](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:212), [rpc.cljc:233](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:233), [rpc.cljc:254](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:254), [rpc.cljc:301](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:301)). Add an explicit, caller supplied positive outstanding limit, with a documented finite default. At the limit, return a local backpressure outcome without allocating an ID or appending a request. Second, “plain apply envelopes” describes the **values on the request and response streams**; the lower `dao.stream.remote` protocol still wraps stream operations in its own id, identity, and outcome envelope ([remote.cljc:48](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:48), [remote.cljc:138](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:138)). It must carry the apply value verbatim.\\n\\n## Target contract\\n\\n| Concern | Ruling |\\n|---|---|\\n| Request | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/op keyword :dao.stream.apply/args vector}`, with arbitrary additional keys. Use `apply/request?` on received values; preserve the entire map when forwarding or retaining it. This is the existing open contract ([apply.cljc:31](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:31), [apply.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38)). |\\n| Success | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/ok value}`; `nil` is a valid value ([apply.cljc:77](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:77), [apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). |\\n| Error | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/error {:dao.stream.apply/code qualified-keyword :dao.stream.apply/message string}}`, with additional envelope or error-map keys permitted ([apply.cljc:63](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:63), [apply.cljc:83](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:83), [apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). |\\n| Validation | rpc’s former `answer?` checks only safe ID and exclusive presence of `ok` or `error`; it does **not** validate the error body. The apply predicate does validate its qualified code and string message ([rpc.cljc:111](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:111), [apply.cljc:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:70)). Use `apply/response?` for decoding, then apply rpc’s safe-ID check at its client boundary. Malformed answers become rpc diagnostics and are consumed once, as its polling path already does ([rpc.cljc:358](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:358), [rpc.cljc:486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:486)). |\\n| Diagnostics and completions | Remain rpc-local events and state, outside the apply wire envelope. They record unsolicited or malformed responses, gaps, terminal loss, and completed work ([rpc.cljc:29](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:29), [rpc.cljc:192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:192), [rpc.cljc:385](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:385), [rpc.cljc:486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:486)). Their public output keys may stay rpc-namespaced; the embedded request and response values must be apply envelopes. |\\n\\nThe field mapping is lossless for application requests and answers: rpc `id/op/args`, `id/ok`, and `id/error/code/message` each have an apply counterpart ([rpc.cljc:71](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:71), [rpc.cljc:99](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:99), [apply.cljc:15](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:15)). The stricter ID domain and the event machinery are client policy, not missing application fields.\\n\\n**IDs.** Leave `apply/correlation-id?` as `some?`: the contract deliberately accepts opaque non-nil IDs and assigns cross-host allocation policy to rpc ([apply.cljc:24](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:24)). rpc continues to mint random integers in its cross-host safe range, reroll collisions against IDs still in use, and fail explicitly after its retry bound ([rpc.cljc:24](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:24), [rpc.cljc:35](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:35), [rpc.cljc:60](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:60), [rpc.cljc:144](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:144), [rpc.cljc:233](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:233)). Document and enforce `safe-id?` and the new outstanding cap **in rpc**, including on received answers. Do not reject another valid apply producer’s opaque ID at the apply layer.\\n\\n**Errors.** Keep transport or client failures as rpc completion reasons unless a server is actually sending an application error response. Define distinct, stable apply-qualified reason words for `not-found`, `detached`, `ended`, `no-surface`, `oversize`, and `transport-error`. Map remote `no-surface` to `:dao.stream.apply/no-surface` and remote `oversize` to `:dao.stream.apply/oversize`; do not collapse them into the generic transport reason. `no-surface`, `oversize`, `not-found`, and `ended` are terminal for that binding; `detached` alone permits rebind; a generic transport failure follows the existing terminal path. The current mapping distinguishes only `not-found` and `channel-gone`, while `rebind` already accepts only `detached` ([rpc.cljc:432](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:432), [rpc.cljc:445](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:445), [rpc.cljc:568](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:568)). The remote source already emits separate `no-surface` and `oversize` protocol errors ([remote.cljc:39](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:39), [remote.cljc:123](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:123), [remote.cljc:164](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:164)).\\n\\n**Identity.** An apply response requires its correlation ID, not a stream identity. Keep it that way: an application envelope can be used over a local pair with no remote identity ([apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). A *remote protocol answer* already names `:dao.stream/identity`; its reflection must accept it only when **both** the ID and identity equal those of the outstanding request. Enforce that in `remote/absorb!` before removing the outstanding entry, marking a reflection gone, filing an answer, installing `more`, or emitting an append outcome. A mismatch is a diagnostic, leaving the legitimate request outstanding. The mirror echoes the request identity, but `absorb!` currently looks up only by ID ([remote.cljc:82](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:82), [remote.cljc:401](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:401)). This is correlation integrity within the remote protocol, not authentication of whoever can write to the channel.\\n\\n## Serving and end-to-end acceptance\\n\\nThe serving composition creates the call-in and call-out media, mints their cursors from their actual handles, registers each handle and declared surface in its remote mirror table, and retains that table, channel, cursor, and any lease for as long as the call can be resumed. The remote mirror serves stream operations; the FFI interpreter reads apply requests and appends apply responses. `apply/serve-once!` already takes caller-owned cursor state, retains a computed response across `full`, and never mints a cursor itself ([apply.cljc:217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:217), [apply.cljc:301](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:301)). The mirror likewise holds only its returned channel cursor and forwards the served stream’s cursors ([remote.cljc:185](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:185)).\\n\\nSpecify the UCF `serve!` callback as **idempotent per live handle within one export binding**: repeated calls return the same identity and channel and refer to the same table entry; retirement or lease reclaim ends that binding explicitly. `lift-frame` can call `serve!` while minting cells and again while lifting entries, so unstable markers would break a single frame ([ucf/remote.cljc:217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:217), [ucf/remote.cljc:435](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:435)). Implement a production composition that owns this registry and its lease lifecycle; the UCF facade only accepts the callback and makes remote descriptors from it ([ucf/remote.cljc:37](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:37), [ucf/remote.cljc:144](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:144)). A refusal returns `nil` before publishing a lift, as the facade specifies ([ucf/remote.cljc:41](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:41)).\\n\\n“VM FFI call answered remotely, end-to-end” is accepted only when a real VM call emits its original apply request; the remote serving composition exposes its call-out request stream to an rpc-driven responder; that responder reads the **unchanged** request, appends a valid apply success or error to the response stream; and the VM resumes or raises the corresponding error through `yin.vm.ffi/call-result`, with the same ID and any extra request keys preserved where the request is retained or transported. Verify pending append retries, response `full`, gap, detach and rebind, terminal not-found, and UCF lift/lower of a retained call. The VM already constructs apply requests and checks apply response correlation ([semantic.cljc:467](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:467), [ffi.cljc:88](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:88)); UCF already carries a retained request envelope verbatim ([ucf/remote.cljc:328](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:328)).\\n\\n## Migration order\\n\\n1. **Contract and driver, one breaking commit.** Replace rpc request and answer constructors, predicates, and accessors with apply equivalents at the wire boundary. Preserve rpc’s client state machine, event names, completion API, ID allocator, and cursor minting. Add the outstanding cap and the distinct error reasons. Migrate rpc tests and the REPL adapter/server tests together. A request emitted by `rpc/request!` and an answer accepted by `rpc/poll!` must pass the apply predicates; rpc must reject an invalid error body and diagnose an unsafe reply ID. Current wire consumers include `yin.repl.adapter` and `yin.repl.serve` ([adapter.cljc:50](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:50), [adapter.cljc:169](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:169), [serve.cljc:424](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:424), [serve.cljc:503](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:503)). `yin.repl.connect` creates and rebinds the driver, and `yin.repl.driver` reads its completion IDs; update their assertions where affected ([connect.cljc:422](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:422), [connect.cljc:516](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:516), [driver.cljc:383](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:383)). The polling observation layer documents rpc and apply as separate state transitions, not separate wire contracts ([observe.cljc:28](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc:28)).\\n2. **Remote correlation and channel loss.** Add the identity check in `remote/absorb!`. Make a pair reader’s in-stream `not-found` or `channel-gone` end that pair binding and propagate loss to the outer link, as it already does for gap and end ([remote_pair.cljc:58](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc:58), [remote_pair.cljc:95](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc:95), [remote.cljc:443](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:443)). Acceptance: a wrong-identity answer cannot complete a request, and a dead pair cannot leave waiters polling forever.\\n3. **Production FFI serving composition.** Supply the stable per-handle `serve!` registry, cursor and reflection ownership, lease retirement, and a remote responder using apply values. Demonstrate the end-to-end VM case above, including UCF retained-envelope round trip.\\n\\nThe correlation check and pair termination are independent remote-core fixes and may land before slice 1. The `no-surface`/`oversize` mapping and outstanding cap belong to convergence’s driver acceptance. Stable `serve!`, leased ownership, and the end-to-end case are required before claiming **remote FFI** complete; they need not block the wire-vocabulary commit.\\n\\nDo **not** rename yin’s `dao.stream.apply/call` syntax or AST: that name is parsed and used throughout the VM ([yin.vm.cljc:194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:194), [semantic.cljc:552](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:552)). Do **not** rebuild VM requests from op and args, tighten apply’s open predicates to rpc’s allocation domain, or add rpc event fields to the application envelope. The VM already emits apply requests, the apply protocol admits additional keys, and UCF explicitly retains the whole envelope because rebuilding it loses those keys ([semantic.cljc:467](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:467), [apply.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38), [ucf/remote.cljc:328](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:328)).\\n\\nVerdict: ADOPTED WITH CHANGESCreated-GMT: 2026-09-28 06:33:18 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-09-28 13:33:18 +0700 (timestamp from the artifact\\nfilename; this header block added at dispatch time by the orchestrator\\nseat, session sess_e57e8e19-522c-4d8b-8cca-5ca89a8e5117)\\nCoding-Agent: glm\\nSession-ID: not-applicable (ZCode subagent; no CLI session id)\\n\\n# Task: one-envelope migration slice 1 — rpc adopts apply envelopes (BREAKING)\\n\\nRole: Stream & Network engineer (dao.stream)\\n\\nImplementers:\\n- Status-Event: 2026-09-28 15:28:15 +0700 | Model: glm-5.3 | Status: failed | Rationale: two ZCode\\n  subagent dispatches by the orchestrator seat (2026-09-28 15:22-15:29\\n  +0700) died at launch with \\\"model concurrency limit exceeded\\\" (19-41s\\n  in; neither edited a file — tree verified clean both times). Third\\n  consecutive GLM subagent cap kill on this host including the prior\\n  seat's slice-2 dispatch; route abandoned for this unit.\\n- Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-09-28 15:28:15 +0700 | Status: active | Rationale: GLM\\n  subagent route systematically blocked by the host concurrency cap;\\n  rerouted to the claude CLI (flat Pro Max, authorized for\\n  implementation; strength table: code migration + complex agentic\\n  coding). Session-ID: ae26f3c6-a6a2-41ad-839e-3f5c615718d8.\\n  Gate must then be non-Claude (reviewer independence).\\n\\nRole: dao.stream engineer. Implement migration slice 1 of the\\none-envelope ruling — read it FIRST, it is the authority:\\ncollab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\\n(sections \\\"Ruling\\\", \\\"Target contract\\\", \\\"Errors\\\", \\\"Migration order\\\"\\nitem 1, and the closing \\\"Do NOT\\\" list).\\n\\nPrerequisite already landed: slice 2 (absorb! identity check, pair\\nend on in-stream not-found/channel-gone) — remote.cljc /\\nremote_pair.cljc; do not touch those files.\\n\\n## The breaking change (one commit, no compatibility shim)\\n\\nReplace rpc's wire vocabulary with apply's at the boundary. rpc keeps\\nits name, its client state machine, event names, completion API, ID\\nallocator (random-safe-id + retry bound + safe-id? check), and cursor\\nminting — only the wire VALUES change:\\n\\n1. Requests: rpc/request! constructs dao.stream.apply/request\\n   envelopes ({:dao.stream.apply/id :op :args}); received request\\n   values are validated with apply/request?.\\n2. Answers: success/error answers become apply's success-response/\\n   error-response shapes ({:dao.stream.apply/id :ok} /\\n   {:dao.stream.apply/id :error {:code :message}}); decode with\\n   apply/response?, then apply rpc's own safe-id? check at the client\\n   boundary. An invalid error body is REJECTED (rpc rejects, not\\n   passes); an unsafe reply id is a DIAGNOSTIC (consumed once, like\\n   the existing malformed path). Preserve the entire envelope map\\n   when forwarding or retaining (the open-envelope / verbatim rule).\\n3. rpc's request-value/request-value?/success-answer/error-answer/\\n   answer?/answer-id/answer-ok/answer-error/answer-ok? either become\\n   thin aliases of the apply equivalents or are retired if unused\\n   after the swap — keep public accessors that yin.repl actually\\n   calls working under the same names.\\n4. Diagnostics and completions stay rpc-local events: public output\\n   keys may stay rpc-namespaced, but every embedded request/answer\\n   VALUE must be an apply envelope.\\n5. Outstanding cap (ruling correction): client-state gains a\\n   caller-supplied positive outstanding limit with a documented\\n   finite default. At the limit, request! returns a local backpressure\\n   outcome — :dao.stream.rpc/backpressure — WITHOUT allocating an id\\n   or appending anything. Pin it.\\n6. Error reasons: transport-error-reason maps remote failures to\\n   DISTINCT stable apply-qualified words — :dao.stream.apply/not-found,\\n   /detached, /ended, /no-surface, /oversize, /transport-error.\\n   Map remote no-surface -> /no-surface and oversize -> /oversize\\n   (never collapsed into transport-error). Terminal for the binding:\\n   not-found, ended, no-surface, oversize; detached alone permits\\n   rebind; generic transport failure follows the existing terminal\\n   path.\\n\\n## Wire consumers to migrate with the change\\n\\nyin.repl.adapter (adapter.cljc ~:50, ~:169), yin.repl.serve\\n(serve.cljc ~:424, ~:503), yin.repl.connect (~:422, ~:516),\\nyin.repl.driver (~:383) — update assertions and value handling where\\naffected. dao.stream.observe documents state transitions, not wire\\ncontracts — should need nothing; verify.\\n\\n## Do NOT (ruling's protected list)\\n\\n- Do not rename yin's dao.stream.apply/call syntax or the AST node.\\n- Do not touch yin.vm.* or dao.stream.apply.cljc (the contract side\\n  needs no change).\\n- Do not touch dao.stream.remote.cljc / remote_pair.cljc (slice 2's).\\n- Do not tighten apply's open predicates; do not add rpc event fields\\n  to the apply envelope.\\n\\n## Tests\\n\\nMigrate test/dao/stream/rpc_test.cljc and the yin.repl\\nadapter/serve/connect/driver tests together with the change. New\\npins: at-limit backpressure (no id allocated, nothing appended, prior\\noutstanding unaffected); no-surface/oversize map to their own words\\nand are terminal; detached alone is rebindable; invalid error body\\nrejected; unsafe reply id -> diagnostic with the request still\\noutstanding; a request emitted by rpc/request! and an answer accepted\\nby rpc/poll! both pass the apply predicates.\\n\\n## Constraints\\n\\n- Run via mise: focused namespaces first, then the full JVM suite;\\n  then the Node lane (npx shadow-cljs compile test + node\\n  target/node-tests.js). All green except the ONE known pre-existing\\n  yin.repl.main-test intermittent (note it explicitly if it fires).\\n- cljstyle check on touched files.\\n- Do NOT git add or commit — orchestrator stages and commits after\\n  the gate.\\n- Report to collab/1790577198000-vm-engineer-one-envelope-slice1.claude-opus-5-5.report.md:\\n  changes with line refs, exact commands + counts, git status.\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-28 15:50 +0700\\nModel: claude-opus-5-5 | Session-ID: ae26f3c6-a6a2-41ad-839e-3f5c615718d8\\n\\n# One-envelope slice 1: rpc adopts apply envelopes (BREAKING). Report\\n\\nStatus: done. All lanes green. Nothing is staged or committed.\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\n### src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc\\n- ns docstring (:1-25): the wire values are now `dao.stream.apply` envelopes. Events, diagnostics, and completions stay rpc-local with `:dao.stream.rpc/*` keys. Safe-id and outstanding-cap policy are documented as client policy. Requires `[dao.stream.apply :as apply2]`, following the repo's alias convention.\\n- `default-max-outstanding` = 64 (:49): the documented finite default.\\n- The request/answer vocabulary (:81-105) is now thin aliases: `request-value`, `request-value?`, `request-id/op/args`, `success-answer`, `error-answer`, `answer-id/ok/error` alias `apply2/request`, `request?`, and so on. The public names yin.repl calls keep working.\\n  - `answer?` (:96) = `apply2/response?` (which validates the error body) plus rpc `safe-id?`.\\n  - `answer-ok?` checks `apply2/ok-key`.\\n- `client-state` (:~120-150): the 4-arity takes opts `{:max-outstanding n}`, which must be a positive integer.\\n  - A non-positive value throws `ex-info`.\\n  - The limit is stored at `:max-outstanding`.\\n- `request!` (:~282-310): new branch after the invalid-request check. When `(count :outstanding)` is at or above `:max-outstanding`, it returns `:dao.stream.rpc/backpressure` with the state unchanged: no id allocated, no unsent request, no append.\\n  - A retained unsent retry is checked before this branch, so it still proceeds.\\n- `answer-diagnostic` (:342): a valid apply response whose id is not `safe-id?` gets diagnostic `:dao.stream.rpc/unsafe-response-id`. Anything else gets `:dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response`, including an invalid error body, which is rejected.\\n  - Used by `normalize-event` (:359) and `handle-event` (:410).\\n  - The existing advance-before-decode rule consumes each diagnostic exactly once.\\n- `transport-error-reason` (:~430-443): adds remote `no-surface` → `:dao.stream.apply/no-surface` and remote `oversize` → `:dao.stream.apply/oversize`.\\n  - Both are terminal, because `rebind` still clears only `/detached`.\\n  - `not-found`, `ended`, and generic `transport-error` are unchanged.\\n- Unchanged: the state machine, event names, completion API and keys, ID allocator (random-safe-id, retry bound, safe-id?), cursor minting, and rebind.\\n\\n### Wire consumers\\n- `src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:117`: `request-outcome` maps `:dao.stream.rpc/backpressure` → `:yin.repl.adapter/backpressure`.\\n  - Before this change, backpressure fell through to `:yin.repl.adapter/transport-result`.\\n  - The driver's default branch publishes it as \\\"request not sent: backpressure\\\".\\n  - `completion-event` (:169) needed no change: it reads rpc-local completion keys and uses the aliased accessors.\\n- `src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:520,524`: the malformed-request path now reads the id through `rpc/request-id`, i.e. the apply id key, instead of the literal `:dao.stream.rpc/id`.\\n  - `evaluate` and `request-value?` need no edits: they go through the aliases, and received requests are now validated by `apply2/request?`.\\n- `yin.repl.connect` (:422, :516): no change. It builds and rebinds the client with the default cap.\\n- `yin.repl.driver` (:383): no change. It reads the rpc-local result key `:dao.stream.rpc/id`.\\n- `dao.stream.observe`: no change. It only names `rpc/poll!` and `apply/serve-once!` in a docstring (:28), with no wire contract.\\n\\n### Tests\\n- `test/dao/stream/rpc_test.cljc`: the existing 13 tests pass unchanged through the aliases. New pins:\\n  - `wire-values-are-apply-envelopes-preserved-whole` (:304): the value emitted by `request!` passes `apply2/request?` and equals `apply2/request`. The answer accepted by `poll!` passes `apply2/response?`, and the whole envelope, with an extra `:trace/hop` key, is kept verbatim in both the result and the completion.\\n  - `an-invalid-error-body-is-rejected-and-leaves-the-request-outstanding` (:323): an unqualified error code gives a `malformed-response` diagnostic. The request stays outstanding and no completion is produced.\\n  - `an-unsafe-reply-id-is-a-diagnostic-consumed-once` (:341): a string-id apply response gives an `unsafe-response-id` diagnostic. The request stays outstanding and the next poll is idle.\\n  - `at-the-outstanding-limit-request-answers-backpressure` (:359): with `{:max-outstanding 1}`, the second request returns backpressure.\\n    - The returned state is `identical?` to the input: no id, no unsent request, the prior outstanding entry untouched.\\n    - The request stream holds only `:op/a`.\\n    - The default cap is applied.\\n    - `{:max-outstanding 0}` throws.\\n  - `no-surface-and-oversize-keep-their-own-terminal-words` (:388): no-surface and oversize map to their own words. `rebind` refuses no-surface, oversize, and not-found; detached alone rebinds.\\n- `test/yin/repl/serve_test.cljc`: error-code assertions now read `:dao.stream.apply/code`, and the malformed-request fixture uses `:dao.stream.apply/id`.\\n- `test/yin/repl/adapter_test.cljc`: the emitted request is compared against `apply2/request`.\\n- The embed, driver, main, and connect tests go through the aliases and are unchanged.\\n\\n## Commands and counts (all via mise)\\n\\n1. Focused JVM: `mise exec -- clj -M:test -n dao.stream.rpc-test -n yin.repl.adapter-test -n yin.repl.serve-test -n yin.repl.connect-test -n yin.repl.driver-test -n yin.repl.embed-test -n dao.stream.observe-test` → **Ran 77 tests, 386 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.**\\n2. Full JVM, first run: `mise exec -- clj -M:test` → 2287 tests, 183331 assertions, **3 failures** in yin.repl.main-test. The Node lane that followed failed `a-node-client-attaches-to-a-dart-server`.\\n   - **Cause of the Dart-peer failures:** `build/yin-repl-peer` is a prebuilt Dart executable (built 10:26 today, before this change). It still spoke the old `:dao.stream.rpc/*` wire vocabulary. This breaking change requires rebuilding it.\\n   - Fix: `mise exec -- bb build:yin-repl-peer`, which compiled and regenerated the exe. This also confirms rpc.cljc compiles under ClojureDart.\\n   - The other two failures, `killing-the-connection-is-observable-and-requests-are-lost` (:608) and `reattaching-resumes-the-served-stream-and-the-deposit-medium` (:638), use a JVM peer from the current classpath. They failed once more in a focused main-test rerun right after the rebuild, then passed in the next focused rerun (18 tests, 134 assertions, 0 failures) and in the full rerun below.\\n   - I am calling these the known yin.repl.main-test intermittent. **Flag:** they failed on two back-to-back runs before going green, so the gate may want to watch them.\\n3. Full JVM, final: `mise exec -- clj -M:test` → **Ran 2287 tests, 183331 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.**\\n   - One loud SKIP, not caused by this change: `a-dart-dialer-reads-a-jvm-server` (build/ws-project-peer absent).\\n4. Node lane: `mise exec -- npx shadow-cljs compile test` (359 files, 0 warnings), then `mise exec -- node target/node-tests.js` → **Ran 2193 tests, 49957 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.**\\n   - The rpc, adapter, and serve test namespaces appear as \\\"Testing …\\\" in the output.\\n5. `mise exec -- cljstyle check` on the 6 touched source and test files: clean.\\n6. `mise exec -- clj -M:kondo --lint` on the same 6 files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\\n\\nLogs: target/slice1-jvm.log (first run), target/slice1-jvm2.log (final), target/slice1-node.log, target/slice1-main-rerun.log.\\n\\n## Protected list: respected\\n- No changes to yin.vm.*, dao.stream.apply.cljc, remote.cljc, or remote_pair.cljc.\\n- The apply predicates were not tightened, and no rpc fields were added to the envelope.\\n\\n## Note for the orchestrator\\nAny Dart peer binary built before this commit (`build/yin-repl-peer`) is stale by construction. Rebuild it (`bb build:yin-repl-peer`, or `bb test`, which rebuilds) before gating the cross-host main-test cases.\\n\\n## git status --short\\n```\\n M docs/orchestrator-log.md            (pre-existing, not mine)\\n M src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc\\n M src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc\\n M src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc\\n M test/dao/stream/rpc_test.cljc\\n M test/yin/repl/adapter_test.cljc\\n M test/yin/repl/serve_test.cljc\\n?? collab/1790577198000-vm-engineer-one-envelope-slice1.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1790577198000-vm-engineer-one-envelope-slice1.prompt.md\\n?? collab/1790577198000-vm-engineer-one-envelope-slice1.claude-opus-5-5.report.md\\n```\\n(`build/yin-repl-peer` was regenerated but is not tracked.)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-stream/1790585138891-reviewer-one-envelope-slice1-gate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:18:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790577198000-vm-engineer-one-envelope-slice1.claude-opus-5-5.report.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Completed-Local: 2026-09-28 15:50 +0700\\nModel: claude-opus-5-5 | Session-ID: ae26f3c6-a6a2-41ad-839e-3f5c615718d8\\n\\n# One-envelope slice 1: rpc adopts apply envelopes (BREAKING). Report\\n\\nStatus: done. All lanes green. Nothing is staged or committed.\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\n### src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc\\n- ns docstring (:1-25): the wire values are now `dao.stream.apply` envelopes. Events, diagnostics, and completions stay rpc-local with `:dao.stream.rpc/*` keys. Safe-id and outstanding-cap policy are documented as client policy. Requires `[dao.stream.apply :as apply2]`, following the repo's alias convention.\\n- `default-max-outstanding` = 64 (:49): the documented finite default.\\n- The request/answer vocabulary (:81-105) is now thin aliases: `request-value`, `request-value?`, `request-id/op/args`, `success-answer`, `error-answer`, `answer-id/ok/error` alias `apply2/request`, `request?`, and so on. The public names yin.repl calls keep working.\\n  - `answer?` (:96) = `apply2/response?` (which validates the error body) plus rpc `safe-id?`.\\n  - `answer-ok?` checks `apply2/ok-key`.\\n- `client-state` (:~120-150): the 4-arity takes opts `{:max-outstanding n}`, which must be a positive integer.\\n  - A non-positive value throws `ex-info`.\\n  - The limit is stored at `:max-outstanding`.\\n- `request!` (:~282-310): new branch after the invalid-request check. When `(count :outstanding)` is at or above `:max-outstanding`, it returns `:dao.stream.rpc/backpressure` with the state unchanged: no id allocated, no unsent request, no append.\\n  - A retained unsent retry is checked before this branch, so it still proceeds.\\n- `answer-diagnostic` (:342): a valid apply response whose id is not `safe-id?` gets diagnostic `:dao.stream.rpc/unsafe-response-id`. Anything else gets `:dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response`, including an invalid error body, which is rejected.\\n  - Used by `normalize-event` (:359) and `handle-event` (:410).\\n  - The existing advance-before-decode rule consumes each diagnostic exactly once.\\n- `transport-error-reason` (:~430-443): adds remote `no-surface` → `:dao.stream.apply/no-surface` and remote `oversize` → `:dao.stream.apply/oversize`.\\n  - Both are terminal, because `rebind` still clears only `/detached`.\\n  - `not-found`, `ended`, and generic `transport-error` are unchanged.\\n- Unchanged: the state machine, event names, completion API and keys, ID allocator (random-safe-id, retry bound, safe-id?), cursor minting, and rebind.\\n\\n### Wire consumers\\n- `src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:117`: `request-outcome` maps `:dao.stream.rpc/backpressure` → `:yin.repl.adapter/backpressure`.\\n  - Before this change, backpressure fell through to `:yin.repl.adapter/transport-result`.\\n  - The driver's default branch publishes it as \\\"request not sent: backpressure\\\".\\n  - `completion-event` (:169) needed no change: it reads rpc-local completion keys and uses the aliased accessors.\\n- `src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:520,524`: the malformed-request path now reads the id through `rpc/request-id`, i.e. the apply id key, instead of the literal `:dao.stream.rpc/id`.\\n  - `evaluate` and `request-value?` need no edits: they go through the aliases, and received requests are now validated by `apply2/request?`.\\n- `yin.repl.connect` (:422, :516): no change. It builds and rebinds the client with the default cap.\\n- `yin.repl.driver` (:383): no change. It reads the rpc-local result key `:dao.stream.rpc/id`.\\n- `dao.stream.observe`: no change. It only names `rpc/poll!` and `apply/serve-once!` in a docstring (:28), with no wire contract.\\n\\n### Tests\\n- `test/dao/stream/rpc_test.cljc`: the existing 13 tests pass unchanged through the aliases. New pins:\\n  - `wire-values-are-apply-envelopes-preserved-whole` (:304): the value emitted by `request!` passes `apply2/request?` and equals `apply2/request`. The answer accepted by `poll!` passes `apply2/response?`, and the whole envelope, with an extra `:trace/hop` key, is kept verbatim in both the result and the completion.\\n  - `an-invalid-error-body-is-rejected-and-leaves-the-request-outstanding` (:323): an unqualified error code gives a `malformed-response` diagnostic. The request stays outstanding and no completion is produced.\\n  - `an-unsafe-reply-id-is-a-diagnostic-consumed-once` (:341): a string-id apply response gives an `unsafe-response-id` diagnostic. The request stays outstanding and the next poll is idle.\\n  - `at-the-outstanding-limit-request-answers-backpressure` (:359): with `{:max-outstanding 1}`, the second request returns backpressure.\\n    - The returned state is `identical?` to the input: no id, no unsent request, the prior outstanding entry untouched.\\n    - The request stream holds only `:op/a`.\\n    - The default cap is applied.\\n    - `{:max-outstanding 0}` throws.\\n  - `no-surface-and-oversize-keep-their-own-terminal-words` (:388): no-surface and oversize map to their own words. `rebind` refuses no-surface, oversize, and not-found; detached alone rebinds.\\n- `test/yin/repl/serve_test.cljc`: error-code assertions now read `:dao.stream.apply/code`, and the malformed-request fixture uses `:dao.stream.apply/id`.\\n- `test/yin/repl/adapter_test.cljc`: the emitted request is compared against `apply2/request`.\\n- The embed, driver, main, and connect tests go through the aliases and are unchanged.\\n\\n## Commands and counts (all via mise)\\n\\n1. Focused JVM: `mise exec -- clj -M:test -n dao.stream.rpc-test -n yin.repl.adapter-test -n yin.repl.serve-test -n yin.repl.connect-test -n yin.repl.driver-test -n yin.repl.embed-test -n dao.stream.observe-test` → **Ran 77 tests, 386 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.**\\n2. Full JVM, first run: `mise exec -- clj -M:test` → 2287 tests, 183331 assertions, **3 failures** in yin.repl.main-test. The Node lane that followed failed `a-node-client-attaches-to-a-dart-server`.\\n   - **Cause of the Dart-peer failures:** `build/yin-repl-peer` is a prebuilt Dart executable (built 10:26 today, before this change). It still spoke the old `:dao.stream.rpc/*` wire vocabulary. This breaking change requires rebuilding it.\\n   - Fix: `mise exec -- bb build:yin-repl-peer`, which compiled and regenerated the exe. This also confirms rpc.cljc compiles under ClojureDart.\\n   - The other two failures, `killing-the-connection-is-observable-and-requests-are-lost` (:608) and `reattaching-resumes-the-served-stream-and-the-deposit-medium` (:638), use a JVM peer from the current classpath. They failed once more in a focused main-test rerun right after the rebuild, then passed in the next focused rerun (18 tests, 134 assertions, 0 failures) and in the full rerun below.\\n   - I am calling these the known yin.repl.main-test intermittent. **Flag:** they failed on two back-to-back runs before going green, so the gate may want to watch them.\\n3. Full JVM, final: `mise exec -- clj -M:test` → **Ran 2287 tests, 183331 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.**\\n   - One loud SKIP, not caused by this change: `a-dart-dialer-reads-a-jvm-server` (build/ws-project-peer absent).\\n4. Node lane: `mise exec -- npx shadow-cljs compile test` (359 files, 0 warnings), then `mise exec -- node target/node-tests.js` → **Ran 2193 tests, 49957 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.**\\n   - The rpc, adapter, and serve test namespaces appear as \\\"Testing …\\\" in the output.\\n5. `mise exec -- cljstyle check` on the 6 touched source and test files: clean.\\n6. `mise exec -- clj -M:kondo --lint` on the same 6 files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\\n\\nLogs: target/slice1-jvm.log (first run), target/slice1-jvm2.log (final), target/slice1-node.log, target/slice1-main-rerun.log.\\n\\n## Protected list: respected\\n- No changes to yin.vm.*, dao.stream.apply.cljc, remote.cljc, or remote_pair.cljc.\\n- The apply predicates were not tightened, and no rpc fields were added to the envelope.\\n\\n## Note for the orchestrator\\nAny Dart peer binary built before this commit (`build/yin-repl-peer`) is stale by construction. Rebuild it (`bb build:yin-repl-peer`, or `bb test`, which rebuilds) before gating the cross-host main-test cases.\\n\\n## git status --short\\n```\\n M docs/orchestrator-log.md            (pre-existing, not mine)\\n M src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc\\n M src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc\\n M 